Detailed breakdown and quick math
1) Income to report
- W-2 wages: $82,350 (report on Form 1040).  
- Consulting (self‑employed): $15,000 — report on Schedule C. You must also file Schedule SE for self‑employment tax.  
- Interest: $1,370 total — report on Form 1040 interest line. (Schedule B is only required if interest > $1,500; you’re under that threshold.)

2) Self‑employment (consulting) — business deductions
- Unreimbursed expenses: If these $1,350 are ordinary/necessary expenses of the consulting business, report them on Schedule C (they reduce self‑employment taxable income). If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (from a W‑2 job), they generally are not deductible for 2018–2025. Clarify which they are.  
- Home office (155 sq ft exclusive). Two methods:

  A. Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction.

  B. Actual method: allocate a share of actual home expenses. You wrote $2,050/month for 780 sq ft (I assume rent). Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600. Business use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%. Allocable rent = $24,600 × 155/780 ≈ $4,890. (If you also pay utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, you can allocate your business percentage of those, too.) Because you rent, you generally cannot depreciate the dwelling; homeowners may depreciate the business portion. If you use the actual method, use Form 8829 to compute and report the deduction.

  Compare: actual method allocable rent ≈ $4,890 vs simplified $775 — actual method is larger here and will reduce your Schedule C net profit more. Note: home‑office deductions cannot exceed the gross income from the business minus other business expenses; any excess may be carried forward. In your numbers the actual deduction is less than your business Gross ($15,000) minus other business expenses ($1,350), so you should be able to take it.

Example net profit calculations (illustrative)
- Start: $15,000 consulting
- Less business expenses (assume these $1,350 are business): → 15,000 − 1,350 = 13,650 before home‑office deduction.

  - If simplified home office ($775): net profit = 13,650 − 775 = $12,875.
  - If actual home office (~$4,890): net profit = 13,650 − 4,890 = $8,760.

- Self‑employment tax (approx): SE tax = 15.3% on ~92.35% of net profit.
  - With actual method net profit $8,760 → SE taxable ≈ $8,093 → SE tax ≈ $1,238 (you deduct half of that (~$619) as an adjustment to income).
  - With simplified method net profit $12,875 → SE taxable ≈ $11,889 → SE tax ≈ $1,818 (half deductible ≈ $909).

This shows the actual method reduces both income tax and SE tax in your situation.

3) Student loan interest ($2,700 paid to MOHELA)
- 2023 maximum student loan interest deduction = $2,500 (above‑the‑line). However there is a MAGI phaseout for single/HOH of $75,000–$90,000. Your combined income (W‑2 + self‑employment + interest) as reported likely puts your MAGI above $90k, so you are probably not eligible for any student loan interest deduction for 2023. If you have other adjustments (traditional IRA contributions, HSA, pre‑tax retirement deferrals already reflected in W‑2 Box 1) that reduce MAGI, it could change eligibility. Also note only $2,500 max is deductible even if you paid $2,700.

4) Charity ($800 to St. Mary’s Food Bank)
- Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). With only $800 charitable and no other major itemized amounts mentioned, itemizing is unlikely to exceed the HOH standard deduction of $20,800. Keep your receipt for substantiation.

5) Itemize vs standard deduction
