Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can walk you through what you can (and can’t) deduct, whether it makes sense to itemize, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll need. I do need one quick clarification before finalizing numbers:

- Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” expenses you incurred for your consulting (self‑employment) work, or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job? (That changes whether they’re deductible.)

Assuming the consulting income is self‑employment income, here’s a clear summary and next steps.

1) Characterize the income
- W‑2 wages: $82,350 (Midwest Health Systems).
- Consulting: $15,000 — treated as self‑employment income (Schedule C) unless you’re an employee of those clients.
- Interest: $890 (Citi) + $480 (Fidelity) = $1,370 — taxable interest reported on Form 1040.
- Total gross before adjustments ≈ $98,720 (82,350 + 15,000 + 1,370).

2) Student loan interest ($2,700 paid)
- The maximum student loan interest deduction for 2023 is $2,500.
- There is an income phase‑out for single/head‑of‑household filers with MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 (full deduction below $75k, phases out to zero at $90k).
- Because your gross income plus self‑employment income likely puts your MAGI above $90,000, you will probably be ineligible for the student loan interest deduction. (If you want, I can compute a more exact MAGI after you confirm whether the $1,350 is business expense and whether you’ll claim the home‑office actual method.)

3) Home office (155 sq ft exclusive use; home = 780 sq ft; rent $2,050/month)
You must meet the regular and exclusive use test (exclusive, regular use as principal place of business) — you stated exclusive, so this likely qualifies if you use it for the consulting work.

Two methods:
- Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction (no Form 8829 needed).
- Actual expense method: compute the business percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9%. Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600; allocable rent = $24,600 × 19.9% ≈ $4,889. You can also allocate utilities, renters insurance, repairs, etc., by the same percentage. Use Form 8829 to calculate and report the actual method.

Which to pick: actual method likely yields a larger deduction here (about $4,889 of rent alone vs $775 simplified). However, the home‑office deduction is a business deduction for Schedule C and cannot create a net unallowed loss for certain passive/activity rules — practically, you should compute both and choose the larger. I can compute both precisely if you give any other home expenses (utilities, insurance, repairs) and confirm the exclusive business use for the consulting.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If these were business expenses for your consulting, they are deductible on Schedule C (not on Schedule A). Combine them with the home‑office deduction to reduce self‑employment net income.
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job, they are no longer deductible for 2018–2025 under current law (miscellaneous itemized deductions suspended), so you cannot deduct them on your federal return.

5) Self‑employment tax and related deductions
- Your consulting net income (gross minus business expenses and home‑office allocation) will be subject to self‑employment tax computed on Schedule SE. You may deduct half of self‑employment tax as an above‑the‑line adjustment on Form 1040 (Schedule 1). That adjustment reduces AGI and thus can affect phaseouts (e.g., for student loan interest), but the effect may not be enough to bring MAGI under the $90k threshold in your case.

Example rough calculation (illustrative):
- If the $1,350 are business expenses and you use the actual home office:
  - Consulting gross 15,000 − business expenses 1,350 − home office allocable rent ≈ 4,889 → net business income ≈ $8,761.
  - Estimated SE tax on that net ≈ $1,238 → half deductible ≈ $619 (above‑the‑line).
  - AGI before student loan interest ≈ 82,350 + 8,761 + 1,370 − 619 ≈ $91,862 (above the $90k phaseout cap).
  - Conclusion: student loan interest likely disallowed; standard deduction likely best (see next section).
- If the unreimbursed expenses were not business expenses, your consulting net would be higher and MAGI would be even higher.

6) Itemize vs. standard deduction (Head of Household)
- Standard deduction for Head of Household in 2023 = $20,800.
- Your listed itemizable amounts: charitable cash $800 (you have receipts), possibly state/local taxes and mortgage interest if any (you didn’t list any). With only $800 of charitable contributions, it’s almost certainly better to take the standard deduction.
- Note: student loan interest is an above‑the‑line adjustment (if eligible) and does not require itemizing. Charitable deductions require itemizing.

Recommendation: You will almost certainly take the standard deduction for 2023 unless you have significant unrevealed deductible items (large state/local taxes, mortgage interest, big medical expenses, or larger charitable gifts).

7) Forms you will use (Federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (e.g., half of SE tax, student loan interest if allowed).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — to report the $15,000 consulting income and any business expenses (including the unreimbursed expenses if they are business related).
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual expense method for the home office (to calculate and report allowed home office expenses). If you use the simplified method, do not file Form 8829 — simply claim the deduction on Schedule C.
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely given amounts you’ve shown).
- Keep Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) from MOHELA, 1099‑INTs from Citi and Fidelity, W‑2 from Midwest Health Systems, and any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from consulting clients (even if you don’t receive 1099s, you must report the income).

8) Arizona state return
