Below I’ll walk through each piece of your situation, show the likely tax treatment, give an example calculation so you can see the effect, list the forms you’ll need, and finish with recommended next steps and a few questions so I can refine the numbers.

Summary (quick):
- You must report W-2 wages ($82,350), consulting self-employment income ($15,000), and interest ($890 + $480 = $1,370) on your 2023 Form 1040.
- Your home office can be deducted against the consulting income (you’re eligible because you have self-employment income). Using the regular method (allocating rent) will almost certainly produce a larger home-office deduction than the simplified method.
- Unreimbursed expenses: if they are business expenses for the consulting work, deduct them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W-2 job, they’re not deductible on the federal return (post-2017 rules).
- Charitable gift ($800) is deductible only if you itemize. The 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household is $20,800 — with only $800 of charitable donations and no other large itemizable amounts provided, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.
- Student loan interest (up to $2,500) is an above-the-line deduction but phases out. With the incomes you reported, you are likely over the AGI/MAGI phaseout threshold for Head of Household ($75,000–$90,000 for 2023), so you may not be eligible. I show a worked example below.
- You will owe self-employment tax on net consulting income (Schedule SE) and can deduct half of that as an adjustment to income.

Details and calculations

1) Interest income
- Citi savings: $890; Fidelity: $480. Total taxable interest = $1,370. Report on Form 1040. (Schedule B is required only if total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500; your total is below that threshold, so Schedule B usually is not required.)

2) Self-employment consulting income and unreimbursed expenses
- Report consulting income and related business expenses on Schedule C.
- If the $1,350 in unreimbursed expenses are related to your consulting work, include them on Schedule C (supplies, travel, phone, etc.). If they were unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, they are not deductible on your federal 2023 return.

3) Home-office deduction
- You stated: home office 155 sq ft exclusive; total home 780 sq ft. Business-use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%.
- Rent = $2,050/month → $24,600/year.

Two methods:
a) Regular (actual expense) method: allocate the business percentage of rent and other home expenses to the home-office deduction. Example for rent only:
  - Rent portion = $24,600 * 19.87% ≈ $4,892 home-office deduction (you’d also prorate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you have them).
  - To claim the regular method, complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach to Schedule C.

b) Simplified method: $5 × square footage (up to 300 sq ft).
  - 155 sq ft × $5 = $775.
  - No Form 8829 required; you enter the simplified deduction on Schedule C.

Given your rent, the regular method (≈ $4,892 from rent alone) will almost certainly yield a larger deduction than the simplified $775. Use regular method unless you prefer the simplicity or lack records for actual expenses.

4) Self-employment tax and effect on AGI
- Home-office deduction and business expenses reduce your net Schedule C profit.
- Self-employment (SE) tax is roughly 15.3% on 92.35% of net SE income. Half of SE tax is deductible above-the-line (Schedule 1) which lowers your AGI.

Example (illustrative) using assumptions:
- Consulting gross: $15,000
- Business expenses (consulting-related): $1,350
- Home-office (regular, rent portion only): $4,892
→ Net self-employment income = 15,000 - 1,350 - 4,892 = $8,758

Compute SE tax:
- Net earnings for SE tax = 0.9235 × $8,758 ≈ $8,087
- SE tax ≈ 0.153 × $8,087 ≈ $1,237
- Half SE tax deductible = $1,237 / 2 ≈ $619

Rough AGI (before student-loan interest deduction):
- W-2 wages: $82,350
- Net SE income: $8,758
- Taxable interest: $1,370
- Subtotal = $92,478
- Less half SE tax adjustment ≈ $619
- AGI ≈ $91,859 (this is the AGI used to determine student loan phaseout eligibility)

5) Student loan interest deduction eligibility
- For 2023, the phaseout for single/head of household is MAGI $75,000 to $90,000. If your MAGI is $90,000 or more, you cannot take the student loan interest deduction.
- In the example above, estimated MAGI ≈ $91,859, which is over $90,000, so the $2,700 student loan interest deduction would be fully phased out.
- If you have additional business expenses or other adjustments that materially reduce your MAGI below $90k, you may be able to take part or all of that $2,700 (the maximum allowable deduction is $2,500; amounts over $2,500 are not deductible—note you reported $2,700 paid, but the maximum deduction is $2,500).

6) Itemize vs standard deduction
- Standard deduction for Head of Household (2023) = $20,800.
- Your provided itemizable amounts: charitable cash gift $800 (you have receipt). You did not mention mortgage interest, property taxes, large state income taxes, or other deductible items.
- Unless you have significant other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state/local tax payments up to the $10k SALT cap, sizable medical expenses exceeding 7.5% of AGI, etc.), the standard deduction will be larger. Based on the info given, you should take the standard deduction.

7) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return).
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — for adjustments and reporting e.g., student loan interest (if eligible) and half SE tax (as an adjustment).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $15,000 consulting income and business expenses (including home-office deduction part if using simplified or Form 8829 result).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular (actual expense) home-office method. If you use the simplified method, you do NOT use Form 8829; you enter the simplified deduction on Schedule C.
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you itemize (seems unlikely given your numbers).
- State return: Arizona Form 140 (Arizona resident return). Arizona typically starts with federal AGI and has its own adjustments, so the federal choices affect state tax.

Other practical notes
- Keep documentation: receipts for the $800 donation (you said you have it), documentation for any business expenses, rent receipts/lease (for home-office allocation), and records showing the exclusive use of the 155 sq ft.
- If you had no estimated tax payments during the year and you owe significant self-employment tax + income tax, you may owe underpayment penalties. Consider adjusting withholding or making estimated payments for 2024.
- If the unreimbursed $1,350 are employee expenses (W-2), they are nondeductible federally for 2023. They might still matter for other purposes (state) — check Arizona rules.

Recommended next steps / questions so I can refine calculations
1. Confirm whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are for the consulting business or for your W-2 job.
2. Do you have any other deductible items not listed (mortgage interest, property tax, state/local tax paid, large medical expenses, unreimbursed employee business expenses explicitly for your W-2 job that might matter for state)?
3. Do you pay utilities, renter’s insurance, or other household costs that we should include in the regular home-office calculation? (Those would increase the home-office deduction.)
4. Do you want me to run a more exact numeric tax estimate (projected federal tax + SE tax + whether student loan interest deduction is allowed) using either the regular or simplified home-office method? If so, I’ll use the details above and recalculate.

If you’d like, I can:
- Recalculate exact AGI/tax owed under both home-office methods and show whether student loan interest is allowed, or
- Prepare a checklist of documents to gather for filing, or
- Estimate your 2024 estimated tax payments.

Tell me which of the follow-ups you want (answer questions 1–3 above and/or ask for a detailed estimate) and I’ll run the numbers.

