Short answer up front
- Most likely you should take the 2023 Head‑of‑Household standard deduction ($20,800) rather than itemizing.  
- Your consulting (self‑employment) income is deductible for business expenses (including a home‑office deduction) on Schedule C; you’ll also owe self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) and can claim the 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995) on that profit.  
- The $2,700 student‑loan interest is probably NOT deductible because your modified AGI (with the consulting income) exceeds the single/HOH phaseout upper limit ($90,000). Confirm exact MAGI to be sure.  
- The $800 charitable gift is only useful if you itemize (but with the standard deduction at $20,800, itemizing likely won’t beat the standard deduction with the numbers you gave).  

Below I explain what to deduct, how to treat the home office, approximate effect on taxable income (actual vs simplified home‑office method), and the forms you’ll need.

1) What you can deduct (and what’s likely NOT deductible)
- Self‑employment (consulting) business expenses: deductible on Schedule C. This includes ordinary & necessary expenses for your consulting work (the $1,350 you listed — if those are business expenses for the consulting work, they are deductible; if they are unreimbursed employee expenses from an employer job, THEY ARE NOT deductible for 2018–2025 under current law). Please confirm the nature of the $1,350.  
- Home‑office deduction: deductible if you use the space regularly and exclusively for the business and you’re self‑employed. See details below.  
- Self‑employment tax: you pay SE tax (Schedule SE) on net self‑employment income; half of that SE tax is an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (reduces AGI).  
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: as a self‑employed person you likely qualify for the 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995) because your taxable income will be well under the QBI wage/phaseout thresholds.  
- Interest income: $890 (Citi) + $480 (Fidelity) = $1,370 — reportable as taxable interest on Form 1040. (Schedule B is not required unless interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500.)  
- Student‑loan interest ($2,700 reported): the maximum allowed deduction is $2,500 in 2023, but it phases out for single/HOH with MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000. Based on the wages + consulting + interest you reported, your MAGI is very likely above $90,000, so you probably cannot deduct this. If you want, give me exact numbers (or I can estimate after we calculate SE tax and half‑SE deduction) and I’ll check precisely. Also keep your 1098‑E from MOHELA.  
- Charitable gift ($800): only deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. With $800 charitable + no large other itemized items provided, itemizing will almost certainly be less favorable than the $20,800 standard deduction for HOH.  

2) Home office — two methods; which to use
You said home office is 155 sq ft exclusive; home is 780 sq ft → business percentage = 155/780 = 19.87% (≈19.9%).

Method A — Actual expenses (Form 8829)
- You allocate direct and indirect home expenses by the business % (19.87%). Rent is an indirect expense that qualifies.  
- Example with rent only: rent $2,050/mo = $24,600/yr → home‑office portion = $24,600 × 19.87% ≈ $4,892. Add other home expenses (utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs) if you want the full allocation. Form 8829 (or Schedule C line for home office with attachment) is used.  
- Limit: the home‑office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from the business. Any disallowed amount may be carried forward (rules apply).

Method B — Simplified method
- $5.00 × actual square feet used (up to 300 sq ft) → 155 × $5 = $775. No Form 8829; you just enter the simplified amount on Schedule C.

Which likely gives a bigger deduction for you?
- Using the numbers you provided, actual method (rent allocation alone) gives about $4,892 vs simplified $775 — actual will be substantially larger and appears preferable, assuming you have no other restrictions. The home‑office deduction reduces your Schedule C net income, hence SE tax and your taxable income.

3) Quick approximate comparison (illustrative)
Assumptions: consulting gross $15,000; business expenses $1,350; interest $1,370; W‑2 wages $82,350; no other adjustments or itemized amounts. I used the SE tax formula (SE tax = 92.35% × net self‑employment income × 15.3%).

- If you use the actual home‑office rent allocation (~$4,892):
  - Net business income ≈ 15,000 − 1,350 − 4,892 = 8,758
  - SE tax ≈ $1,238 (half ≈ $619 deductible)
  - QBI ≈ 20% of 8,758 ≈ $1,752 (deduction)
  - Rough taxable income after standard deduction and QBI ≈ ~$69,300 (ballpark)

- If you use the simplified method ($775):
  - Net business income ≈ 15,000 − 1,350 − 775 = 12,875
  - SE tax ≈ $1,819 (half ≈ $910 deductible)
  - QBI ≈ 20% of 12,875 ≈ $2,575
  - Rough taxable income after standard deduction and QBI ≈ ~$72,300 (ballpark)

Conclusion from the example: actual method (using rent allocation) looks materially better for you (roughly $3,000 lower taxable income in this illustrative calculation). Exact result depends on other home expenses you can allocate and exact SE tax calculation; but likely choose actual method.

4) Student loan interest detail (important)
- 2023 phaseout for student‑loan interest deduction: single/HOH MAGI $75,000–$90,000. If your MAGI is over $90,000, the deduction phases out to zero. With W‑2 wages of $82,350 plus self‑employment income and interest, your MAGI (before the student loan interest deduction itself) is very likely > $90,000 — so you likely cannot claim the $2,700 as an adjustment. If you want, I can compute MAGI precisely using the exact net self‑employment income (after you confirm whether the $1,350 are business expenses and whether other adjustments exist).

5) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.  
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (e.g., half of SE tax, student loan interest if allowed). (Some adjustments now appear on 1040 lines, but Schedule 1 is used when required.)  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $15,000 consulting income and business expenses (including home office if using simplified method entry here or the figure from Form 8829 if using actual).  
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.  
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for the home office. If you use the simplified method, you do NOT file 8829.  
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — to claim the QBI (20%) deduction. With your income level, simplified Form 8995 is likely sufficient.  
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (likely you will NOT itemize).  
- Schedule B — only if interest + dividends > $1,500 (your interest is $1,370 so probably not needed).  
- Keep: W‑2 (Midwest Health Systems), 1099‑NEC or records for consulting income (even if you didn’t get 1099s), 1099‑INTs from Citi and Fidelity, 1098‑E from MOHELA (student loan interest statement), receipts for charitable donation, documentation for home office and business expenses.

6) State return (Arizona)
- You will also file Arizona state income tax (Form 140, or e‑file equivalent). Arizona generally starts from federal AGI with its own adjustments — check state rules for deductions/credits. The home‑office deduction flows through from federal Schedule C. Confirm Arizona filing details or ask me to pull the specific Arizona forms/lines if you want.

7) Other practical things / suggestions
- Confirm whether the $1,350 is self‑employment business expense. If it’s unreimbursed employee expenses from an employer job, it’s nondeductible.  
