Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer up front
- Most likely you should take the standard deduction for 2023 (head of household = $20,800) unless you have additional itemizable deductions (state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or big charitable gifts) that you didn’t list and that push your itemized total above $20,800.
- Your consulting income is reported on Schedule C and you can claim a home office deduction (you qualify — exclusive and regular use). If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for the consulting work, they are deductible on Schedule C; if they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible (suspended through 2025).
- Student loan interest: the maximum deduction is $2,500, but it phases out for single/head‑of‑household filers with MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 in 2023. With your W‑2 + consulting + interest income you’re likely above $90k and therefore likely ineligible for the student loan interest deduction — but see the questions below so I can confirm.
- Forms you’ll commonly use: Form 1040, Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business), Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax), Schedule 1 (adjustments), Form 8829 (home office — only if you use the actual expense method), Schedule A (only if you itemize), and Form 8995 (or 8995-A) for the QBI deduction if applicable. Also include any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC and 1099‑INTs you received.

Details and how each item is handled

1) Income to report
- W‑2 wages from Midwest Health Systems: report on Form 1040 (you’ll get a W‑2).
- Consulting: report gross receipts on Schedule C. If you have 1099‑NEC forms from clients include them; even if you did not get 1099s you must report the income.
- Interest from Citi and Fidelity: taxable interest reported on Form 1040 (and you’ll get 1099‑INTs).

2) Home office (you said 155 sq ft exclusive; total 780 sq ft; rent $2,050/month)
- You appear to meet the “regular and exclusive use” test for a home office used for your consulting business, so you can claim a home office deduction for the self‑employment activity.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction. No Form 8829 required; enter on Schedule C.
  - Actual method: prorate actual expenses by the office share (155/780 ≈ 19.87%). Example for rent: $2,050 × 12 = $24,600 × 0.1987 ≈ $4,890 of rent allocable to the office. You may also prorate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. (If you own the home, depreciation would also be a factor; for renters depreciation generally does not apply.)
- Limitations: home office deduction cannot create or increase a net loss from that business (some parts can be carried forward if limited). If your consulting net income before the home office deduction is small, the deduction may be limited.
- Where to report: simplified method amount is entered on Schedule C (line for business use of home); with actual expenses you complete Form 8829 and transfer the allowable amount to Schedule C.

3) Business expenses and “unreimbursed expenses”
- If the $1,350 are expenses related to your consulting activity (supplies, software, travel, phone, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C and reduce self‑employment income.
- If the $1,350 are unreimbursed expenses from your W‑2 employer, they are not deductible (the miscellaneous itemized deduction for unreimbursed employee expenses is suspended through 2025).

4) Self‑employment tax and adjustments
- You will owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net self‑employment earnings (computed on Schedule SE). SE tax is roughly 15.3% of net earnings (92.35% of net profit × 15.3%).
- Half of the SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (Schedule 1), which reduces AGI.
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction (Section 199A) on up to 20% of qualified business income from your Schedule C — claim on Form 8995 (simplified) if you qualify.

5) Student loan interest ($2,700 paid)
- The maximum deduction for 2023 is $2,500. It phases out for single/head‑of‑household filers with MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 (full phaseout at $90k).
- Rough estimate: your gross income (W‑2 82,350 + consulting 15,000 + interest 1,370) ≈ $98,720 before adjustments. Even after subtracting half of SE tax and other above‑the‑line items, your MAGI is likely above $90,000, so the student loan interest deduction will likely be disallowed. I can run a tighter estimate if you confirm whether the $1,350 are business expenses and whether you’ll use simplified or actual home office method.

6) Charitable donation ($800 to St. Mary’s Food Bank)
- Cash gift to a qualified charity is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). There is no universal above‑the‑line charitable deduction for 2023 for non-itemizers. Because the standard deduction for HOH ($20,800) is much larger than $800, the $800 won’t tip you into itemizing unless you have additional itemizable deductions (state tax, mortgage interest, property tax, large medical expenses, etc.).

7) Whether to itemize or take the standard deduction
- Standard deduction for head of household (2023) = $20,800.
- Your known itemizable items: $800 charitable. Unless you have substantial additional itemizable amounts (e.g., more than ~$20k in SALT, mortgage interest, or large medical expenses), the standard deduction will be higher.
- Recommendation: take standard deduction unless you have other itemizable items you didn’t list.

8) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (federal return).
- Schedule 1 (additional income / adjustments).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for consulting income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — only if you use the actual-expense method for the home office; not required for the simplified method.
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize.
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — for the QBI deduction (likely Form 8995, the simplified form, if eligible).
- Keep your 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC (if any) and 1099‑INTs for interest.
- Arizona state return (AZ Form 140 or the correct AZ form for your situation) — state rules differ.

Action steps I suggest
1. Confirm two quick items:
   - Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the consulting work, or unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job?
   - Do you have any other sizable deductible items (mortgage interest, state/local taxes > $10,000 combined, large medical expenses, other charitable gifts)?
2. Decide home office method:
   - If you want simplicity, use the simplified method ($775). If your rent/utilities allocated to the office substantially exceed $775 and you have good records for utilities and other household expenses, consider the actual method (you’ll need Form 8829).
3. Collect documents: W‑2, any 1099s, 1099‑INTs, receipts for business expenses, home rent and utility bills (if using actual method), charitable receipt for the $800, and student loan interest statement (Form 1098‑E) from MOHELA.
4. If you want, I can run an estimated computation of AGI, taxable income, approximate federal tax, and show whether the student loan deduction phases out — but I’ll need confirmation of whether the $1,350 is a business expense and which home office method you prefer.

Would you like me to run an estimated calculation (AGI → taxable income → estimated tax), using the simplified home‑office method and assuming the $1,350 are business expenses?

