Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer up front
- Most likely you should take the 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household ($20,800) rather than itemize, unless you have significant other itemizable deductions (state income or property taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses).
- You can (and should) deduct the home-office expenses for your consulting work because you have an exclusive 155 sq ft office and self‑employment income.
- Your consulting income and expenses go on Schedule C; you’ll also need Schedule SE (self‑employment tax). The home‑office deduction can be claimed either by the simplified method or by the regular/actual‑expense method (regular method likely gives a larger deduction in your situation).
- The student‑loan interest deduction may be limited or eliminated by your income (phaseout for single/HOH in 2023 is MAGI $75,000–$90,000). With the numbers you gave, you’re likely above the phaseout unless additional business deductions lower your MAGI.
- Forms you’ll likely need: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8829 (if using the regular/home‑office actual expense method), and Form 8995 (QBI simplified form) if you claim the 20% qualified business income deduction. Possibly Schedule A only if you choose to itemize (unlikely).

Details and how I reached those conclusions

1) Income to report
- W‑2 wages: $82,350 (report on Form 1040).
- Self‑employment (consulting) income: $15,000 — report on Schedule C (and you’ll receive/should receive 1099‑NEC if clients issued them).
- Interest: $890 (Citi) + $480 (Fidelity) = $1,370 — report as taxable interest on Form 1040. (Schedule B only required if interest > $1,500, so not required here.)

2) Home‑office deduction
- Qualification: You described an exclusive, regular use 155 sq ft office in a 780 sq ft home and you have self‑employment income — that qualifies.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 × 155 sq ft = $775 (easy, no Form 8829).
  - Regular/actual: allocate a share of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, depreciation) by business‑use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.9%.
    - Example with rent only: annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600 × 19.9% ≈ $4,888 — larger than the simplified $775, so the regular method is probably better if you can allocate utilities/other costs as well.
- How to report:
  - If using regular method, complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and transfer the allowable deduction to Schedule C.
  - If using simplified, compute $5 × sq ft and enter on Schedule C (no Form 8829).

3) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If these are expenses for your consulting business (e.g., supplies, travel for clients, software, phone usage for business), they belong on Schedule C and reduce net business income.
- If they are unreimbursed expenses related to your W‑2 job, they are no longer deductible as miscellaneous itemized deductions (suspended through 2025). So confirm whether they are consulting expenses.

4) Self‑employment tax and QBI
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax; you calculate SE tax on Schedule SE. Half of your self‑employment tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1 and reduces AGI.
- You may be eligible for the 20% Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) on your net consulting profit. For 2023 you can generally use Form 8995 if your taxable income is below the QBI thresholds (for single/HOH threshold ≈ $182,100). If under the threshold, the 20% deduction is straightforward.

5) Student‑loan interest ($2,700)
- The student loan interest deduction is up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income, but it phases out for MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 for single/HOH in 2023.
- Quick estimate using the numbers you gave:
  - Gross income = 82,350 + 15,000 + 1,370 = $98,720.
  - Even after subtracting half of SE tax and typical business deductions, your MAGI is likely > $90,000, so the student‑loan interest deduction will probably be fully phased out.
- Bottom line: you need to compute MAGI after allowable above‑the‑line adjustments (including half SE tax). If your MAGI ends up ≤ $90,000 you’ll be eligible for a (possibly reduced) portion of the $2,700.

6) Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household = $20,800.
- Your itemizable amounts you listed: charitable $800, possibly state income tax withholding or property tax (you didn’t list), medical expenses (only deductible over 7.5% of AGI), mortgage interest (you did not list), etc.
- With only $800 charitable and no mortgage interest/property tax listed, itemizing will be far less than $20,800 — so take the standard deduction unless you have substantial other itemizable deductions (not listed here).

7) Forms you will likely file (federal)
- Form 1040.
- Schedule 1 (for adjustments like student‑loan interest deduction and the deduction for one‑half of self‑employment tax).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report consulting income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — compute SE tax and the deductible half.
- Form 8829 (if using the regular/home‑office actual expense method). If you use the simplified home‑office method, you don’t use Form 8829.
- Form 8995 (or Form 8995‑A) for the QBI deduction if applicable (Form 8995 is the simple worksheet if you’re below the threshold).
- Schedule A only if you itemize (unlikely based on information given).
- Schedule B not required (interest < $1,500).

8) State return
- You’ll also file Arizona individual return (Form 140 for full‑year residents). Arizona has its own rules for deductions and adjustments; check state rules for student loan interest and business income.

9) Documentation to keep
- W‑2(s) and 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for consulting.
- 1099‑INT from Citi and Fidelity.
- Receipts for charitable donations (you said you have receipt for St. Mary’s Food Bank — keep it).
- Records for home‑office calculation: floor plan, measurements, rent receipts, utility bills, renter’s insurance, repairs — keep invoices/receipts.
- Records of the $1,350 in expenses showing they are business‑related (if you intend to deduct them).
- Student loan interest statement (Form 1098‑E from MOHELA) showing interest paid.

10) Action items / questions for you (so I can give a precise recommendation)
- Are the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses for the consulting business (self‑employment) or for your W‑2 job?
- Do you have any other itemizable deductions for 2023 (state income tax paid, property tax, mortgage interest, large medical expenses)?
- Do you want me to run a numerical estimate of taxable income using either (a) simplified home office or (b) actual/regular home office method? If yes, please provide monthly utilities and any other home expenses you want included (electric, internet, renter’s insurance, repairs).
- Did you receive 1099‑NEC forms from the consulting clients? (You must report the income even if they didn’t issue 1099s.)

A quick numeric example (illustrative)
- Home‑office % = 155/780 ≈ 19.9%.
- Rent allocation (if only rent is used): $2,050 × 12 = $24,600 × 19.9% ≈ $4,888 (regular method) vs simplified $775.
- If consulting business expenses = $1,350 + home‑office $4,888, net consulting income ≈ $8,762. Self‑employment tax on that net is ≈ $1,239 (Schedule SE), half ≈ $619 deductible above the line. Using these sample numbers your MAGI would remain above $90k, which likely disallows the student‑loan interest deduction — but a small change in business deductions could change that.

Final note
If you want, I can:
- Run a step‑by‑step worksheet using the exact numbers you confirm (specify whether the $1,350 is business related and any utilities/other home costs) and show estimated AGI, taxable income, self‑employment tax, and whether the student‑loan deduction applies.
