Short answer up front
- You will almost certainly be better off taking the 2023 standard deduction for head of household ($20,800) rather than itemizing based on the details you gave.
- Your home-office deduction is allowed for your consulting (self-employment) income. The actual-method deduction (allocating rent/utilities) is much larger than the simplified-method here (see numbers below) — that reduces your Schedule C net profit and your self‑employment tax.
- The student loan interest deduction (up to $2,500) is very likely phased out for you in 2023 because your MAGI will be above the phaseout range for head of household ($75,000–$90,000).
- Key forms you’ll use: Form 1040, Schedule C (profit/loss from business), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), and either Form 8829 (if you use the actual home‑office method) or Schedule C entry (if you use the simplified method). Use Schedule A only if you decide to itemize (unlikely based on provided items).

Details, calculations, and items you can/can’t deduct

Income to report
- W‑2 wages: $82,350 (report on Form 1040).
- Consulting (self‑employment) income: $15,000 (report on Schedule C).
- Interest: $890 (Citi) + $480 (Fidelity) = $1,370 (report on Form 1040). Because total interest < $1,500, you do not need Schedule B.

Home‑office deduction (self‑employment only)
Requirements: the home space must be used regularly and exclusively for business and be your principal place of business (or used to meet clients). From what you wrote, it sounds like you meet the test for your consulting.

Two options:
1) Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 (no depreciation, no Form 8829).
2) Actual method (allocate real rent/expenses): you rent and pay $2,050/month → annual rent = $24,600. Office percent = 155 / 780 = 19.87%. Rent portion deductible = 24,600 × 19.87% ≈ $4,890. (You could also prorate utilities, renters insurance, etc., by the same percent if you have records. Because you rent, you do not claim depreciation for the dwelling.)

Which to pick? Actual method yields a significantly larger deduction (~$4,890 vs $775). Use Form 8829 to compute/claim the actual home‑office deduction if you choose actual.

Self‑employment expenses and “unreimbursed expenses”
- If your $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for the consulting work (supplies, travel, phone, software, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C.
- If the $1,350 are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible for federal income tax for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).
Tell me which these are and I’ll fold them into the math.

Student loan interest ($2,700 to MOHELA for years 2016–2019)
- The maximum student loan interest deduction is $2,500 in 2023, but it phases out by MAGI. For single/head‑of‑household the phaseout range is $75,000–$90,000. Your combined income (W‑2 + consulting + interest) is about $98,720 before adjustments, so you are above the phaseout upper limit and therefore very likely not eligible to claim any of the student loan interest deduction for 2023.
- Exact phaseout depends on MAGI calculations (adjustments such as half of SE tax reduce AGI), but with the amounts you gave you’re almost certainly over the limit. Keep your 1098‑E(s) for records anyway.

Unreimbursed employee expenses
- As noted above: not deductible if they relate to your W‑2 employment.
- If they relate to your consulting business, they belong on Schedule C (see earlier).

Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for head of household = $20,800.
- Your only itemizable amount you listed is charitable $800 (receipt) plus any state/local taxes you paid (you didn’t list these). Even adding a few thousand in state tax likely won’t exceed $20,800. So standard deduction is almost certainly better.
- If you have significant mortgage interest, large property taxes, or other big itemizable items not listed, re-run the comparison; otherwise choose the standard deduction.

Estimated numbers (to illustrate effects)
Assumptions: total ordinary income = 82,350 (W‑2) + 15,000 (consulting) + 1,370 (interest) = 98,720.

I’ll show two representative scenarios (assume the $1,350 is deductible business expense — that’s the most favorable case for reducing AGI):

A) Actual home‑office method + $1,350 business expenses
- Home‑office deduction (rent portion) ≈ $4,890
- Net self‑employment profit = 15,000 − 4,890 − 1,350 = $8,760
- Self‑employment tax (approx) ≈ $1,238; deductible half ≈ $619
- AGI ≈ 98,720 − 619 = 98,101 → above $90k → student loan interest phased out.

B) Simplified home‑office + $1,350 business expenses
- Home‑office deduction = $775
- Net SE profit = 15,000 − 775 − 1,350 = $12,875
- SE tax ≈ $1,820; half deductible ≈ $910
- AGI ≈ 98,720 − 910 = 97,810 → above $90k → student loan interest phased out.

If the $1,350 are NOT business expenses the numbers change slightly but you remain above the $90k threshold in realistic scenarios — same conclusion about student loan interest.

Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report consulting income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — ONLY if you use the actual method for the home office (to compute the allowable portion of rent/utilities).
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (probably not).
- Keep 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC or records of consulting income, 1099‑INTs for the two interest amounts, W‑2 from Midwest Health Systems, and Form 1098‑E from MOHELA showing student loan interest (even if you can’t deduct it).
- Form 1040‑ES — if you expect similar self‑employment income for the current year, use this to make estimated tax payments (you may owe quarterly estimated taxes for Social Security/Medicare and income tax on your consulting income).

Arizona state return
