Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer up front
- Most likely you will take the 2023 head‑of‑household standard deduction ($20,800) rather than itemizing, because the only clear itemized amount you listed is $800 in charitable donations and you didn’t list mortgage interest, large state/property tax, or other substantial itemizable items.
- The $2,700 student‑loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (not an itemized deduction) but is subject to income phase‑outs; with the income numbers you gave you will probably be over the phaseout and may get little or none of that deduction.
- Your $15,000 consulting income is self‑employment income. Deductible business expenses (including a home‑office deduction) go on Schedule C; you’ll also owe self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). The home‑office deduction can be claimed either by the simplified method ($5 × 155 sq ft = $775) or the actual‑expense method (your portion of rent/utilities/etc. ≈ 155/780 of those expenses — see example below).
- Forms you will likely need: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments/other income), Schedule C (profit/loss from self‑employment), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Form 8829 if you use the actual home‑office method (or Form 8995 for the QBI deduction if you claim it). For Arizona state return use AZ Form 140 (resident).

Details and calculations

1) Standard deduction vs itemizing
- 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household = $20,800.
- Itemized deductions would include charitable contributions ($800), mortgage interest, state/local taxes (SALT, capped at $10,000), large medical expenses (only the portion over 7.5% of AGI), etc.
- With only $800 of clear charitable contributions and no big mortgage interest or property taxes provided, the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger. If you have other itemizable amounts (significant state income or property tax paid, mortgage interest, large unreimbursed medical expenses), add them up and compare to $20,800.

2) Student‑loan interest ($2,700)
- The deduction limit is $2,500 (or the amount you actually paid if less) and is an adjustment on Schedule 1, not part of Schedule A.
- Phaseout for single/head‑of‑household in 2023: MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000. Over $90,000 => phased out completely.
- Your rough MAGI based on the numbers you gave: 82,350 (W‑2) + 15,000 (consulting) + 890 + 480 (interest) ≈ $98,720, which is above the $90k phaseout. That suggests you probably will not be able to claim the student‑loan interest deduction unless you have other pre‑AGI adjustments (traditional IRA deduction, HSA contributions, etc.) that reduce MAGI below the threshold. Also you need Form 1098‑E or loan statements to support the interest claimed.

3) Interest income
- Citi $890 + Fidelity $480 = $1,370 total interest. Because total taxable interest is under $1,500 you typically don’t need Schedule B; you must include the interest on Form 1040.

4) Consulting income and self‑employment tax
- Report consulting gross receipts on Schedule C. Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (including home‑office, supplies, mileage, etc.). Net profit flows to Form 1040 and is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE).
- You can deduct one‑half of self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

