Quick summary / answer up front
- Filing status: Head of household — good.
- Standard deduction (2023 HOH): $20,800. Based on the items you listed, you’ll almost certainly take the standard deduction rather than itemize.
- Student loan interest ($2,700): probably not deductible because your income (after accounting for the consulting) is above the phase‑out; you likely exceed the upper MAGI limit for the deduction.
- Home office: You can claim a home‑office deduction for your self‑employment if you use the 155 sq ft exclusively and regularly for the consulting business. The regular method (allocating part of your rent) will give a MUCH larger deduction than the simplified method.
- Forms you will need: Form 1040, Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Form 8829 (or the simplified method) for home office (if using regular method), Schedule 1 (adjustments to income), and Schedule A only if you decide to itemize (unlikely). Also report interest income from bank/Broker 1099‑INTs and include AZ Form 140 for state return.

Detailed calculations and reasoning
1) Gross income (as you reported)
- W‑2 wages: $82,350
- Consulting (self‑employment) gross: $15,000
- Interest: Citi $890 + Fidelity $480 = $1,370
Total gross before adjustments = $98,720

2) Home‑office deduction (regular method)
- Home office: 155 sq ft; home = 780 sq ft → business percentage = 155 / 780 = 19.87% (≈19.9%).
- Rent: $2,050/month → annual rent = $24,600.
- Rent portion allocable to home office = $24,600 × 19.87% ≈ $4,890.
(If you also pay utilities, renter’s insurance or other direct/indirect home expenses, those would also be apportioned — that would increase the allowed deduction. The regular method uses Form 8829 to split direct/indirect expenses.)

Simplified method alternative: 155 sq ft × $5 = $775. Since $4,890 >> $775, use the regular method (Form 8829) unless you prefer the simplicity of the $775 flat amount.

3) Business expenses and net self‑employment income
- Consulting gross: $15,000
- Subtract business expenses: $1,350 (you said unreimbursed expenses — assuming these are business expenses for the consulting)
- Subtract home‑office portion of rent allocated to business (≈ $4,890)
Net profit for Schedule C ≈ 15,000 − 1,350 − 4,890 = $8,760

4) Self‑employment tax (approximate)
- Net subject to SE tax uses 92.35% of Schedule C net: 8,760 × 0.9235 ≈ 8,090
- SE tax ≈ 8,090 × 15.3% ≈ $1,238
- You can deduct half of SE tax (≈ $619) as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

5) Effect on AGI and student loan interest deduction
- AGI before student loan interest = W‑2 (82,350) + Schedule C net (8,760) + interest (1,370) = 92,480
- Subtract half SE tax (~619) → adjusted AGI ≈ 91,861
- For tax year 2023 the student loan interest deduction phases out for single/head‑of‑household filers between $75,000 and $90,000 MAGI (and is eliminated above the upper limit). With estimated MAGI ≈ $91.9k you are above the upper limit so the student loan interest deduction is likely disallowed. (If you have other above‑the‑line adjustments that lower MAGI below $90k, you may qualify for a partial deduction — if you want I can re‑run the numbers with any additional adjustments.)

6) Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household = $20,800.
- Your listed potential itemized deductions: charitable gifts $800 (deductible only if you itemize); you did not list mortgage interest or significant state/local taxes (and SALT is capped at $10k). So your itemizable deductions based on what you gave are far less than $20,800.
- Conclusion: take the standard deduction.

7) What to report and which forms to file
- Federal:
  - Form 1040
  - Schedule 1 (for adjustments — e.g., half self‑employment tax, student loan interest if eligible)
  - Schedule C (report consulting income and business expenses)
  - Schedule SE (calculate self‑employment tax)
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the regular home office method. If you prefer simplified method, don’t file 8829; enter the simplified amount on Schedule C.
  - Schedule A only if you itemize (unlikely)
  - Schedule B only if your taxable interest and dividends exceed $1,500 or you have certain foreign accounts (your interest total $1,370 < $1,500 → usually no Schedule B needed)
- State:
  - Arizona individual income tax return (Form 140 for full‑year residents). Arizona rules for deductions differ from federal in some ways; report Arizona taxable income per AZ instructions.
- Information returns to watch for: 1099‑NEC from each client (if they issued it for >$600) and 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV from bank/broker for the interest. Even if you don’t receive a 1099, you must report all income.

8) Practical recordkeeping and documentation
- Keep receipts / bank statements for the $800 charitable donation (you said you have a receipt).
- Keep invoices, receipts and logs for the $1,350 business expenses and any direct home‑office expenses.
- Keep rent/utility bills and a sketch or floorplan showing the exclusive office area and size (155 sq ft) for substantiation.
- Keep MOHELA statements or Form 1098‑E for the student loan interest paid in case you need to show amounts (but again, likely not deductible because of MAGI).

9) Other notes and tax planning
- Because you have self‑employment income, you owe self‑employment tax in addition to income tax. If your withholding on your W‑2 was low, you might owe a balance when you file or could owe estimated taxes for future years.
- If you have additional deductible items you didn’t list (mortgage interest, big state taxes, unreimbursed medical >7.5% of AGI, large charitable gifts, etc.) that change the itemize vs standard decision, tell me and I’ll re‑evaluate.
- If you prefer, I can run a fuller estimated tax computation (federal + Arizona) and estimate what you’ll owe or get refunded, if you provide withholding amounts from Form W‑2 and any estimated payments.

