Summary answer (short): You will almost certainly take the standard deduction (Head of Household = $20,800 for 2023). Your consulting income must be reported on Schedule C and will be subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). The home‑office deduction is available only for the self‑employment work and can be taken either by the simplified method ($5/sq ft = $775 for 155 sq ft) or the actual‑expense method (your share of rent, utilities, etc.; you’d generally use Form 8829 for actual expenses). Your $800 cash gift to St. Mary’s is only deductible if you itemize (and you must have the charity acknowledgement since it’s >$250). The student‑loan interest deduction likely phases out at your income level. See details, calculations, and next steps below.

1) What you must report
- W‑2 wages: report on Form 1040 (wages as shown on Form W‑2).
- Consulting income ($15,000): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If net profit > $400, you must also file Schedule SE to compute self‑employment tax.
- Interest income ($890 Citi + $480 Fidelity = $1,370): report as taxable interest on Form 1040 (you should receive 1099‑INTs).

2) Home office — qualification and options
- It can be deducted only for the self‑employment consulting activity (not for your W‑2 employee work).
