Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether to itemize vs. take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, and which federal (and state) forms you’ll need. I’ll also show quick example calculations so you can see the tax impact. A couple of clarifying questions are below — answer those and I can refine the numbers.

Quick clarifying questions
1. Is the $2,700 listed the amount of student-loan interest you paid in tax year 2023 (and did MOHELA issue a 1098‑E for 2023)? The student‑loan interest deduction only applies to interest paid in the tax year you’re filing for.
2. Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses related to the consulting (self‑employment) work, or were they unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job? (Employee unreimbursed expenses are not deductible for 2018–2025.)
3. Do you have other itemizable deductions (state income tax or real estate tax paid, mortgage interest, medical expenses, large charitable gifts other than the $800, etc.)?

Summary of deductions and treatment (rules and how they apply to your situation)
- Standard deduction (2023): As head of household the 2023 standard deduction is $20,800. This is large, so most taxpayers with only a modest amount of itemizable deductions will take the standard deduction.
- Charitable contribution ($800): Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). You must keep the receipt — you have it, so that’s good.
- Student‑loan interest ($2,700 reported): Student‑loan interest is an ABOVE‑THE‑LINE deduction (reported on Schedule 1) up to $2,500, subject to a MAGI phaseout. For 2023 the phaseout for single/HOH starts at $75,000 and fully phases out at $90,000 MAGI. If your 2023 MAGI is $90,000 or more, you cannot claim the deduction. (If you paid > $600 in 2023, MOHELA should issue Form 1098‑E showing interest paid.)
- Interest from savings ($890 + $480 = $1,370): Taxable interest; report on Form 1040. Schedule B is required only if interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500; you are below that threshold so Schedule B is not required.
- Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350): If these are business expenses for your consulting activity (self‑employment), they belong on Schedule C and reduce your net self‑employment income. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (for your W‑2 job) they are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.
- Home office (155 sq ft exclusive; home 780 sq ft; rent $2,050/mo): If your consulting is self‑employment and you meet the “exclusive and regular use” test (you said it’s exclusive), you can claim a home‑office deduction on Schedule C. You can choose:
  - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft -> 155 * $5 = $775 deduction.
  - Regular (actual) method: prorate actual household expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance) by the percentage of the home used for business (155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%). Rent alone would give approx 24,600 * 19.87% ≈ $4,888. If you have utilities, renters insurance, etc., the deductible amount under the actual method will will likely be larger than the simplified method in your case. To use the actual method you complete Form 8829 (or report the appropriate portion on Schedule C — Form 8829 is used to compute the allowable deduction when you’re self‑employed).
  - You cannot claim the home office deduction for a W‑2 workplace unless you are an eligible reservist or certain other narrow exceptions apply (generally not allowed for employees after TCJA).
- Self‑employment tax and deductions: Net self‑employment income is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You may deduct one‑half of your self‑employment tax as an above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: If you have net qualified business income from your consulting, you may be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995) subject to limits. With relatively small net self‑employment income, you’re likely to get the 20% QBI deduction (but it depends on your final taxable income).

Example numerical illustrations (estimates) — show why these choices matter
Assumptions: W‑2 wages = $82,350; consulting gross = $15,000; interest = $1,370; assume the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are NOT business expenses (i.e., not deductible). I show two home‑office options.

1) If you use the actual method for the home office (rent portion only used here):
- Rent annual = $2,050 * 12 = $24,600
- Home office % = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%
- Home office deduction ≈ $24,600 * 19.87% ≈ $4,888
- Net self‑employment income ≈ $15,000 − $4,888 = $10,112
- Self‑employment tax (approx) ≈ 15.3% * 92.35% * 10,112 ≈ $1,430; half SE tax ≈ $715 (above‑the‑line)
- AGI ≈ 82,350 + 1,370 + 10,112 − 715 ≈ $93,117
- MAGI ≈ AGI (roughly) > $90,000, so student‑loan interest deduction would be phased out (not allowed).
- Standard deduction $20,800 is almost certainly better than itemizing (your only known itemizable amount is $800 charity).
- QBI (20% of net business income) ≈ 20% * $10,112 ≈ $2,022 additional deduction (Form 8995).

2) If you use the simplified home‑office method:
- Home office deduction = $775
- Net SE income ≈ $15,000 − 775 = $14,225
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% * 92.35% * 14,225 ≈ $2,010; half SE tax ≈ $1,005
- AGI ≈ 82,350 + 1,370 + 14,225 − 1,005 ≈ $96,940
- Again AGI > $90,000 — student loan interest would be phased out.
- QBI ≈ 20% * 14,225 ≈ $2,845

Bottom line from those examples: using the actual method for the home office (with rent prorated) will likely produce a significantly larger deduction than the simplified method and lower your taxable income. Even so, with your W‑2 wage level the student‑loan interest deduction is likely phased out because your MAGI appears to be at or above the $90,000 threshold. The $800 charitable donation by itself is unlikely to push you over the standard deduction threshold, so you’ll most likely take the standard deduction.

Forms you will typically need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual tax return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — student‑loan interest deduction (if eligible) and deduction for half of SE tax go here
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $15,000 consulting income and business expenses (home office deduction if you choose simplified, or other business expenses)
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — computes your SE tax and the deductible half
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — use this if you choose the actual method for home‑office allocation (not needed for the simplified method)
- Form 8995 (Qualified Business Income Deduction) — for claiming the 20% QBI deduction if your taxable income is below thresholds (simpler form for lower incomes)
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize (e.g., you have large state taxes, mortgage interest, or other itemizable items exceeding $20,800)
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) — MOHELA should issue this if you paid interest; use it to support a deduction if eligible
- Form 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (if received from clients) — report on Schedule C even if you did not receive a 1099

Arizona state return
- Arizona Form 140 (resident) or the correct AZ return — report income and state deductions per Arizona rules.

Records to keep
- 1098‑E from MOHELA, 1099‑INTs, any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC
- Rent receipts and utility bills (for actual home office method) and your square footage calculation
- Receipts/invoices for business expenses (if the $1,350 or other amounts are business related)
- Charitable donation receipts (you already have the receipt for the $800)
- Any other documentation supporting state or other deductions

Recommendations / next steps
1. Confirm whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses were for your consulting business (self‑employment). If they were business expenses, include them on Schedule C — that will reduce net SE income and could change MAGI and student‑loan deduction eligibility.
2. Confirm that the $2,700 is interest paid in 2023 and whether you received Form 1098‑E. If it is interest paid in 2023, we’ll check MAGI to see if any portion of the $2,500 allowable deduction is available.
3. In most cases here you will:
   - Claim the home‑office deduction on Schedule C (actual method looks better because of high rent; use Form 8829)
   - File Schedule C and Schedule SE
   - Take the standard deduction on Form 1040 (unless you tell me about other large itemized deductions)
   - Likely not be eligible for student‑loan interest deduction because your MAGI will probably exceed $90,000, but I can recompute if you provide clarifications (especially whether the $1,350 is a business expense).
4. If you want, give me the answers to the clarifying questions above. I can then:
   - Recompute AGI and show precisely whether you can claim the student‑loan interest deduction
   - Show the exact tax difference between simplified vs actual home‑office methods
   - Create a checklist of forms and line items to file or drafts of the Schedule C entries

Would you like me to run the numbers again assuming the $1,350 is a business expense (and assuming the $2,700 is interest paid in 2023)? I can also prepare a short checklist you can give to a tax preparer or use to prepare your own return.

