Below is a clear, actionable summary of how your 2023 items should be treated, what’s likely best for you (standard vs itemize), how the home‑office works, estimated impacts on AGI/student‑loan phaseout, and which forms you’ll need.

Short bottom line
- You’ll report: W-2 wages, consulting (self‑employment) income on Schedule C, and interest from savings on Form 1040 (from 1099‑INTs).
- Your best bet is almost certainly to take the 2023 Head‑of‑Household standard deduction ($20,800) — your itemizable deductions shown ($800 charitable + no other big items given) are far below that.
- The student‑loan interest “deduction” (max $2,500) is phased out for single/HOH filers with MAGI $75,000–$90,000 for 2023. Based on the incomes you gave, your MAGI will likely exceed $90k, so you probably cannot take that deduction for 2023 unless you take other steps to lower MAGI (see options below).
- Home‑office: you can claim it because you said it’s used exclusively and regularly for the consulting work. You can choose either the simplified or actual method; the actual method (allocating rent by square footage) appears to give you a larger deduction this year.

Details, calculations, and reasoning

1) Income to report
- W‑2 from Midwest Health Systems: $82,350 — report on Form 1040.
- Consulting: $15,000 — report on Schedule C (self‑employment income). Report full gross regardless of whether you got a 1099‑NEC.
- Interest: $890 (Citi) + $480 (Fidelity) = $1,370 — report as interest income (1099‑INTs).

2) Business expenses / unreimbursed expenses
- If the $1,350 are expenses for your consulting work (not unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job), treat them as Schedule C business expenses. If they are unreimbursed expenses from an employer (W‑2 job), they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025.
- For self‑employment you will also owe self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) and can deduct one‑half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income.

3) Home‑office deduction (you gave: 155 sq ft exclusive; home = 780 sq ft; rent = $2,050/mo)
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction.
  b) Actual: business percentage = 155 / 780 = 19.87%. Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600. Rent portion deductible = 24,600 × 0.1987 ≈ $4,888. (Plus you could allocate and deduct business share of utilities, renters’ insurance, etc., if you have them.)
- Form filing: If you use the actual method, complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C. If you use the simplified method, you do not file Form 8829 — enter the simplified amount on Schedule C.

Which method is better for you: using only rent as the home expense, the actual method (~$4,888) is substantially larger than the simplified $775. Use actual method if you’re comfortable documenting rent/utilities; simplified is easier.

4) Effect on AGI and student‑loan interest deduction (estimate)
- Student‑loan interest rules: maximum deduction $2,500 (adjustment to income). For single/HOH, the deduction phases out for MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 in 2023; if MAGI ≥ $90,000 the deduction is disallowed.
- Quick estimates (rounded):

  Using actual home‑office (rent share ≈ $4,888):
  - Business expenses = $1,350 + $4,888 = $6,238
  - Net business profit ≈ $15,000 − $6,238 = $8,762
  - Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × 8,762 ≈ $1,238 → half SE deduction ≈ $619
  - Rough AGI ≈ 82,350 (W‑2) + 8,762 (net business) + 1,370 (interest) − 619 (half SE tax) ≈ $91,863

  Using simplified home‑office ($775):
  - Business expenses = $1,350 + $775 = $2,125
  - Net business profit ≈ $12,875
  - SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × 12,875 ≈ $1,819 → half ≈ $910
  - Rough AGI ≈ 82,350 + 12,875 + 1,370 − 910 ≈ $95,685

- Conclusion: In either case your MAGI/AGI is above $90k, so the student‑loan interest deduction would be fully phased out for 2023. (Note: the QBI (20%) deduction is computed after AGI and will not reduce MAGI used for the student‑loan phaseout.)

5) Charitable contribution
- $800 cash to St. Mary’s Food Bank is an itemized deduction on Schedule A. Because the standard deduction for HOH in 2023 is $20,800, the standard deduction will nearly certainly be more beneficial unless you have other large itemizable items (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, large medical expenses above the threshold, etc.). Based on the info you gave, take the standard deduction.

6) Self‑employment tax, estimated payments, and QBI
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax (calculated on Schedule SE) on your net consulting profit.
- You may need to have made quarterly estimated tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) during 2023 if tax owed from the consulting activity exceeded safe withholding thresholds.
- You will likely be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction of up to 20% of qualified business income. That reduces your taxable income (not AGI) and is claimed via Form 8995 (simple) or Form 8995‑A (complex).

7) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for certain adjustments (e.g., student‑loan interest if eligible, other adjustments).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report consulting income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax and deduction of one‑half SE tax.
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual method for home‑office deduction.
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — for the QBI deduction.
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (not likely here).
- Arizona resident return (AZ Form 140 or the correct AZ form for 2023) — state filing required.
- Keep your 1099‑INTs and any 1099‑NEC (or other 1099s) you received and the 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) from MOHELA.

Documentation to keep
- Receipts for charitable donation (you said you have it) — keep the acknowledgement.
- 1098‑E (MOHELA) showing interest paid in 2023.
- Receipts / records for the $1,350 business expenses and any utilities/rent statements to support your home‑office allocation.
- Records of estimated tax payments.

Options to consider if you want the student‑loan deduction
- Lower MAGI for 2023 by making deductible retirement plan contributions that reduce AGI: e.g., SEP‑IRA or Solo 401(k) contributions (available for self‑employed income) or a deductible Traditional IRA if you’re eligible. Note: deductions for employer‑sponsored plan participants have their own phaseouts; confirm eligibility. A SEP or Solo 401(k) contribution can substantially reduce self‑employment taxable income and might bring MAGI below the $90k threshold — consult a tax pro before implementing late in the year.
- Contribute to an HSA (if you have a high‑deductible health plan) — reduces AGI.

Next steps / offer
