Short answer first
- Most likely take the 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household ($20,800). Your likely federally itemizable deductions (an $800 charity receipt only) are far smaller than the standard deduction.
- You cannot take the student‑loan interest deduction because your income (about $98.7k before adjustments) is above the phaseout (the deduction phases out for single/HOH MAGI between $75k and $90k for 2023).
- You do have a deductible home‑office expense for your consulting (self‑employment) income. Using the actual method (Form 8829) looks better for you than the simplified method — it produces a materially larger deduction and also lowers your self‑employment tax.
- Forms you will need: Form 1040 (federal), Schedule C (self‑employment income/expenses), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Form 8829 (if you use the actual home‑office method), Form 8995 (QBI deduction) or 8995‑A if required, Schedule 1 (for adjustments such as half SE tax), plus any 1099‑INT, 1099‑NEC and your W‑2. If you itemize (unlikely) you would use Schedule A.

Details, calculations, and assumptions
1) Income summary (what you provided)
- W‑2 wages: $82,350
- Self‑employment (consulting) gross: $15,000
- Interest: $890 (Citi) + $480 (Fidelity) = $1,370
- Total gross before any business adjustments: $98,720

2) Student‑loan interest
- You paid $2,700 student‑loan interest. The student‑loan interest deduction (up to $2,500) is an above‑the‑line adjustment for eligible taxpayers, but it phases out for single/head‑of‑household filers with MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 in 2023 and is eliminated above $90,000.
- With your income (~$98.7k before adjustments) you’re above the $90k cutoff, so you cannot take the student‑loan interest deduction.

3) Charitable contribution
- $800 cash donation to St. Mary’s Food Bank is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Because the HOH standard deduction in 2023 is $20,800, a single $800 donation will not make itemizing beneficial unless you have much larger additional itemizable amounts (state & local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.). Recommendation: take the standard deduction.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- Important clarification needed: are these expenses related to your self‑employment (consulting) or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from an employer?
  - If they are business expenses for your consulting work, they are deductible on Schedule C (not on Schedule A) and reduce your self‑employment net income.
  - If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (you incurred them as an employee), miscellaneous itemized deductions for unreimbursed employee expenses are suspended for tax years 2018–2025 and are not deductible on your federal return.

5) Home office (you provided: 155 sq ft exclusive use; home 780 sq ft; rent $2,050/mo)
- You meet the basic requirements (exclusive and regular use, used as principal place of business for your consulting) so you can take a home‑office deduction for the portion of rent/utilities attributable to the office.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
     - 155 sq ft × $5 = $775 deduction.
  b) Actual method: allocate actual home expenses to the business by percentage of square footage.
     - Business percentage = 155 / 780 = 19.8718% (≈19.87%).
     - Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600.
     - Rent portion attributable to business = $24,600 × 19.8718% ≈ $4,887.69.
     - If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, other home costs you can allocate the same way and increase the deduction.
- Which to choose? Based on rent alone, the actual method (~$4,888) gives a much larger deduction than the simplified ($775). The actual method is likely better for you.
- Form to use: Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to compute and report the actual home‑office deduction (the final amount flows to Schedule C). If you use the simplified method you do not use Form 8829 — you simply report the simplified amount on Schedule C.

6) Self‑employment tax, QBI and impacts of the home‑office choice (approximate numbers)
(These are examples to show the relative tax effects; exact numbers can vary with other deductions/withholdings.)

Assume the $1,350 is a business expense (Schedule C). Two scenarios:

A. Actual home‑office (use Form 8829)
- Net business profit = $15,000 − $1,350 (other business expenses) − $4,887.69 (home office) = $8,762.31
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × net = 0.153 × 0.9235 × 8,762.31 ≈ $1,238
- Deductible half of SE tax ≈ $619 (an adjustment to income on Schedule 1)
- QBI (20% of qualified business income) ≈ 20% × 8,762.31 ≈ $1,752 (reported via Form 8995)
- Result: lower net business income, lower SE tax. This scenario generally produces lower overall federal tax for you.

B. Simplified home‑office ($5/sq ft)
- Net business profit = $15,000 − $1,350 − $775 = $12,875
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 0.153 × 0.9235 × 12,875 ≈ $1,819
- Half SE tax ≈ $909 (adjustment)
- QBI ≈ 20% × 12,875 = $2,575
- Because the actual method produces a larger business deduction, it tends to both reduce SE tax and lower taxable income overall; in your numbers the actual method is the better option.

7) Recommendation
- Take the 2023 Head‑of‑Household standard deduction ($20,800).
- Treat the consulting income as self‑employment: file Schedule C and Schedule SE.
- Use the actual home‑office method with Form 8829 (based on the rent numbers you gave, actual method looks materially better than the simplified).
- Report interest income on Form 1040 (include 1099‑INT amounts).
- Do not expect to be able to claim the student‑loan interest deduction because your income is above the phaseout.
- If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are NOT business expenses but employee expenses, they are not deductible on your 2023 federal return — if they are business expenses for your consulting, include them on Schedule C.

8) Forms and documents you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (federal return)
- W‑2 from Midwest Health Systems
- Schedule 1 (adjustments such as the deductible half of SE tax)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your consulting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (if using actual home‑office method)
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A if required) for QBI deduction
- Schedule A only if you decide to itemize (unlikely)
- 1099‑INT(s) for the bank and Fidelity interest
- Any 1099‑NEC or records of consulting payments (you must report the income even if you did not receive 1099s)
- Receipts/support: charity receipt (St. Mary’s), records of rent payments, utility bills, documentation showing square footage measurement of the office, receipts for business expenses, student loan interest statement (Form 1098‑E if issued) — keep these for your files

9) State (Arizona)
- You’ll also file an Arizona individual return (Arizona form for residents, such as AZ Form 140 — check the current AZ form name/version). Arizona conformity to federal items varies; you may want to double‑check state treatment of the student loan interest and other items with the AZ instructions or a tax preparer.

10) Next steps / questions for you
