--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 7533 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer up front
- Most likely you should take the 2023 head‑of‑household standard deduction ($20,800) — your itemizable items as stated (~$800 charitable) are far below that threshold unless you have substantial additional deductible state/local taxes or mortgage interest you haven’t mentioned.
- Your consulting income must be reported on Schedule C (you can deduct legitimate business expenses there). The home‑office deduction is available for your consulting (self‑employment) income; actual method (about $4,890) will give a larger deduction than the simplified method ($775), but it must be claimed against the self‑employment business and cannot exceed that business’s net income after other business expenses.
- The $1,370 interest from Citi + Fidelity is taxable interest and should be reported on Form 1040 (and on Schedule B only if your total interest > $1,500 or other Schedule B triggers apply).
- The student‑loan interest deduction may not be available because of the income phaseout — I need one clarification (below).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (TCJA). If the $1,350 are business expenses for your consulting (self‑employment), they belong on Schedule C and are deductible there.

Details and calculations
1) Income you must report
- W‑2 wages: $82,350 (report on Form 1040 using your W‑2).
- Self‑employment (consulting): $15,000 — report on Schedule C; pay self‑employment tax on net profit (Schedule SE).
- Interest: $890 (Citi) + $480 (Fidelity) = $1,370 — report on Form 1040; Schedule B only if > $1,500 or other reasons.

2) Home office
- Size: 155 sq ft exclusive use; home = 780 sq ft.
- Business percentage: 155 / 780 = 19.87% (≈19.9%).
- Rent: $2,050/month → annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600.
- Actual‑expense allocation for home office = 19.87% × $24,600 ≈ $4,889.85 (round to $4,890). That is the portion of rent you can allocate to the business when using the actual‑expense method. If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., you can also allocate the business percentage of those costs.
- Simplified method: $5 × 155 sq ft = $775.
- Which to choose: the actual method (Form 8829) yields ~$4,890 vs $775 for simplified, so actual gives a larger deduction — but actual method requires Form 8829 and recordkeeping and the deduction cannot exceed net profit from the consulting business. If your consulting net income (before the home‑office deduction) is less than the allocated home‑office amount, the deduction is limited; excess may carry forward (see Form 8829 rules).

3) Student loan interest ($2,700 reported)
- Important: only interest you actually paid in 2023 is deductible on the 2023 return. If your $2,700 figure refers to interest paid in prior years (2016–2019), it cannot be deducted on your 2023 return.
- If $2,700 is interest paid in 2023, the maximum student‑loan interest deduction is generally $2,500 (but phaseouts apply).
- Phaseout: for 2023 the student‑loan interest deduction begins to phase out for single/head‑of‑household at MAGI $75,000 and is fully phased out at $90,000. Your preliminary gross income (82,350 + 15,000 + 1,370 = $98,720) is above the phaseout ceiling, so you probably cannot take this deduction unless you reduce MAGI via adjustments (e.g., deductible retirement contributions where allowed). So likely no student‑loan interest deduction for 2023.

4) Charitable contribution
- Cash gift of $800 to a qualified public charity: deductible only if you itemize. Since standard deduction for HOH = $20,800 and your stated itemized items are much smaller, you will probably NOT itemize and thus will not get a federal deduction for this $800 (it still helps for state return only if you itemize on state, depending on AZ rules).
- Keep the receipt.

5) Unreimbursed expenses $1,350
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job: not deductible on federal 2018–2025 (suspended).
- If they are ordinary and necessary business expenses for your consulting (self‑employment), report them on Schedule C (that reduces self‑employment income).

6) Self‑employment tax / adjustments
- Net profit from consulting (Schedule C) is subject to income tax and self‑employment tax (~15.3% on net SE earnings). You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income.
- Retirement contributions for the self‑employed (SEP IRA, solo 401(k)) are deductible and can reduce AGI and self‑employment tax — useful for both tax savings and potentially restoring eligibility for the student‑loan interest deduction. (Rules and limits vary; I can estimate possible contributions if you want.)

7) Itemize vs standard deduction
