Short answer up front
- You should report the $15,000 consulting on Schedule C (self‑employment). The consulting income and the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses (if they are business expenses for the consulting) and the home‑office deduction all reduce your self‑employment net profit.
- The home‑office deduction is available (you have an exclusive 155 sq ft home office). The actual (regular) method will almost certainly give a bigger deduction than the simplified method because your rent is high.
- Your student‑loan interest deduction is likely NOT allowed because your 2023 MAGI (with the numbers you gave) is above the phaseout range for head‑of‑household (phaseout ends at $90,000 for 2023).
- Your $800 cash gift to St. Mary’s is deductible only if you itemize. With the numbers you gave, the standard deduction for head of household ($20,800 for 2023) will almost certainly be larger than your itemized total, so you’d take the standard deduction.
- Forms you’ll typically need: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8829 (if using the regular home‑office method), Schedule 1 (adjustments), Form 1098‑E (student loan interest), Form(s) 1099‑INT (interest), and Arizona state return (AZ Form 140). See details below.

Details and quick math (approximate)
1) Income summary (your numbers)
- W‑2 wages: $82,350
- Consulting (self‑employment): $15,000
- Interest: $890 (Citi) + $480 (Fidelity) = $1,370
- Total income = $98,720

2) Home office
- Home size = 780 sq ft; office = 155 sq ft → business % = 155/780 ≈ 19.9%.
- Simplified method: 155 sq ft × $5 = $775.
- Regular (actual) method: allocate the business percentage to home expenses. Your rent is $2,050 × 12 = $24,600; 19.9% × $24,600 ≈ $4,888 of rent is deductible as part of business use of home. (If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, include the business share of those too.)
- Recommendation: use the regular/actual method here — it gives a much larger deduction (≈$4,888 vs $775). If you use the regular method you generally complete Form 8829 and carry the result to Schedule C.

3) Business expenses and self‑employment tax (approx)
- If you use actual home‑office: business expense total = $1,350 + $4,888 = $6,238 → net self‑employment profit ≈ $8,762.
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% on 92.35% of net profit:
  - SE taxable amount ≈ 0.9235 × 8,762 ≈ $8,096
  - SE tax ≈ 0.153 × 8,096 ≈ $1,239
  - Deduction for half SE tax (above the line) ≈ $620
- If you use simplified home‑office: net profit would be higher and SE tax higher; the actual method is still likely better in your case.

4) Student‑loan interest ($2,700)
- The student‑loan interest deduction (up to $2,500) phases out for single/head‑of‑household filers with MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 (2023). With the rough AGI above (even after half SE tax), your MAGI is above $90,000, so the student‑loan interest deduction is likely disallowed.
- Important: the deduction is for interest actually paid in 2023 and you should have Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer. If the $2,700 is not paid in 2023 or those payments were in earlier years, that will change things — please confirm the tax year of those payments.

5) Charitable gift ($800)
- Only deductible if you itemize on Schedule A. With your figures, itemized deductions (charity $800 plus any state income/property tax, mortgage interest, etc.) are very likely < $20,800 standard deduction for head of household, so taking the standard deduction will be better. If you have significant state taxes or mortgage interest you didn’t mention, re‑check itemizing.

6) Interest reporting
- Combined interest $1,370 — below the $1,500 threshold that requires Schedule B, so you can just report it on Form 1040.

7) Which deductions apply whether or not you itemize
- Business expense deductions (Schedule C) and the home office deduction (Schedule C/Form 8829 or simplified on Schedule C) are allowed regardless of whether you itemize or take the standard deduction. The student‑loan interest deduction (if allowed) is an above‑the‑line adjustment. Charitable deductions are only on Schedule A (so only if you itemize).

8) Documents / forms you will need (Federal)
- Form 1040 (2023)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report $15,000 and business expenses including unreimbursed business expenses if they are business‑related, and the home‑office deduction result
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method for the home office
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) — to support student loan interest paid (if you claim it)
- Form(s) 1099‑NEC or other documentation for consulting income (report income even if you did not receive a 1099)
- Form(s) 1099‑INT for the interest
- Schedule A (if you choose to itemize — probably not advantageous with the amounts shown)
- Arizona state return (AZ Form 140) and any state schedules as required

Other items & planning ideas
- If you want to try to qualify for the student‑loan interest deduction, you could explore ways to reduce MAGI (e.g., deductible retirement contributions such as a SEP IRA or Solo 401(k) for self‑employment income). Those contributions can also reduce taxable/self‑employment income; whether it’s worthwhile depends on exact numbers and limits.
- Make sure the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are actually business expenses for the consulting work. If these are unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job, they’re not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.
- Because you’ll owe self‑employment tax and maybe additional income tax, check estimated tax payments or increased withholding to avoid penalties.

What I need from you (optional) to be more precise / produce a worksheet
- Confirm whether the $2,700 student‑loan interest was paid in tax year 2023 (and whether you received Form 1098‑E for 2023).
- Confirm that the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for the consulting (not employee expenses).
- Tell me whether you have mortgage interest, property tax, or state income tax paid in 2023 (or total state tax withheld on W‑2).
- Tell me whether the consulting clients issued 1099‑NEC(s).
If you want, I can run a more detailed worksheet (exact AGI, taxable income, estimated federal tax and self‑employment tax) using your answers or draft a list of items to give to your tax preparer.

