Detailed guidance and estimates

1) Which deductions you can actually take (federal)
- Home office: deductible only for self‑employment. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 × 155 sq ft = $775 (no Form 8829; easier).
  - Regular method: allocate actual home expenses by business square footage. Your business percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%. Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600 → rent portion ≈ $4,888. If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., the allocable share of those is also deductible. If you use the regular method you generally file Form 8829 (Business Use of Your Home) with Schedule C.
  - Which to use? Regular method usually gives a larger deduction here (about $4,888 from rent alone vs $775 simplified). Use the method that gives the bigger benefit and keep receipts if you use regular.
- Business expenses: the $1,350 — if those are expenses related to consulting (supplies, mileage, software, etc.) put them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses, they are not deductible.
- Self‑employment tax: you will owe SE tax (15.3% on net earnings after the 7.65% adjustment). Half of SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040.
- Student loan interest: only the interest actually paid in 2023 can be considered. Even if you paid $2,700, the deduction is capped at $2,500 and phases out; for Head of Household the phaseout range is MAGI $75k–$90k (if your MAGI > $90k you cannot claim it). Based on the income you listed, your MAGI will likely exceed $90k — so you probably cannot deduct this for federal purposes. (You should keep the 1098‑E from MOHELA in case your MAGI is lower once all adjustments are calculated.)
- Charitable gift $800: deductible on Schedule A only if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction you cannot separately deduct this (no “above‑the‑line” charitable deduction for 2023 for most taxpayers).

2) Quick numeric illustration (approximate)
(Assumes the $1,350 and home office are business expenses for the consulting.)
- Self‑employment income: $15,000.
- Home office (regular method): ~ $4,888 (rent portion only). + business expenses $1,350 → total business expenses = $6,238 → net profit = $8,762.
  - Net earnings subject to SE tax = $8,762 × 92.35% ≈ $8,096 → SE tax ≈ $1,240 → half deductible ≈ $620.
  - AGI contribution from self‑employment = $8,762 − $620 ≈ $8,142.
- Interest income = $1,370.
- W‑2 income = $82,350.
- Approx AGI ≈ $82,350 + $8,142 + $1,370 = $91,862 (rough). Because AGI is over $90k, the student loan interest deduction will likely be disallowed.
- Standard deduction (HOH) = $20,800. Itemizing would require your total Schedule A deductions to exceed $20,800 — with only $800 charitable and unknown state taxes, that’s unlikely.

If you use the simplified home office:
- Home office = $775; business expenses $1,350 → total = $2,125 → net profit = $12,875 → SE tax ≈ $1,819 (half deductible ≈ $909) → AGI effect roughly less favorable than regular method. So regular method is nearly certainly better here.

3) Forms you’ll need (Federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (if additional income/adjustments apply) — often generated automatically by tax software
