Below I’ll walk through what’s deductible, whether to itemize vs. take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, and which federal (and a note about state) forms you’ll use for 2023. I’ll flag places where I made reasonable assumptions and ask a couple of quick clarifying questions at the end.

Summary of what you told me (used below)
- Filing status: Head of Household (Phoenix, AZ)
- W-2 wages: $82,350 (Midwest Health Systems)
- Self‑employment (consulting) gross: $15,000 (2 clients)
- Interest: Citi savings $890 + Fidelity $480 = $1,370
- Home: rent $2,050/month → $24,600/year; total home size 780 sq ft; exclusive home office 155 sq ft
- Expenses/costs you reported: $2,700 student loan interest (MOHELA, 2016–2019), $800 cash charitable gift (receipt), $1,350 unreimbursed expenses

1) Which items are deductible (federal) and where they go
- W-2 wages: reported on Form 1040 (from your W-2).
- Interest income ($1,370): report on Form 1040. (Schedule B is only required if total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 — you are under that.)
- Self-employment income (consulting): report gross and business expenses on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Business expenses that are ordinary and necessary for the consulting work (e.g., software, supplies, professional fees, part of your “unreimbursed expenses” if those relate to consulting) are deductible on Schedule C. Note: unreimbursed employee expenses (if those were from W-2 employment) are not deductible on individual returns anymore.
- Home office:
  - If the space is used exclusively and regularly for the business, you can take a home-office deduction. You have two methods:
    - Simplified method: $5.00 × square feet used, up to 300 sq ft. For 155 sq ft that is $775.
    - Actual expense method: multiply the business-use percentage of the home by actual allowable home expenses (rent, utilities portion, renter’s insurance portion, etc.). Your business-use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%. Example: rent $24,600 × 19.87% ≈ $4,887 (utilities/insurance could add more if you allocate portions). Because you rent, depreciation is generally not applicable to a rental residence you don’t own, but rent & utilities are deductible portions.
  - The home-office deduction is taken on Schedule C (and if you use actual method you document it on Form 8829).
  - Note: the home-office deduction cannot increase or create a net loss from the business (it’s limited to business income).
- Self-employment tax: you pay self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self-employment earnings; this is computed on Schedule SE. Half of your self‑employment tax is an “above-the-line” deduction (i.e., reduces AGI) reported on Schedule 1.
- Student loan interest: the student loan interest deduction can be up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1) but is phased out based on your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI). For 2023, the phaseout for single/head of household is MAGI $75,000–$90,000; above $90,000 generally eliminates the deduction. With the numbers you provided, your MAGI is likely above that phaseout threshold (see calculations below), so you probably cannot claim the student loan interest deduction — but see clarifying Qs if you have other adjustments or retirement contributions that may lower MAGI.
- Charitable contribution ($800 to St. Mary’s Food Bank): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction (see next section), you cannot deduct this donation on your federal return. Keep the receipt for your records.
- Unreimbursed expenses: If these are for your consulting (self-employment) work, include them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses tied to a W‑2 job, they are not deductible for federal returns (suspended by TCJA through 2025).

2) Itemize vs. standard deduction (recommendation)
- 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household = $20,800.
- Likely itemizable amounts you listed: charitable $800. You did not list mortgage interest, property tax, large state tax payments, or medical expenses above 7.5% of AGI.
- Based on your provided items, your likely itemized total (≈ $800) is much less than $20,800 → you should take the standard deduction for 2023.
- Exception: if you have other itemizable deductions you didn’t list (large state income tax or property tax, mortgage interest, large unreimbursed medical expenses, or other deductible items), please tell me and I’ll re-evaluate.

3) How to treat the home office (practical guidance)
- Eligibility: you already stated the space is exclusive home office (155 sq ft) — that meets the exclusive/regular use test for a home office for a self-employed person.
- Choose a method:
  - Simplified method: easier recordkeeping; gives $775 for 155 sq ft. Use Form 8829 is not required; you report it on Schedule C (line for home office simplified amount).
  - Actual method: larger deduction in your case because your rent is high — estimated rent portion ≈ $4,887/year (24,600 × 155/780). You must keep rent receipts and utility bills and complete Form 8829 to document the allocation (Form 8829 is for business use of home; the form is filed with your tax return). Actual method gives a larger deduction but requires better records.
- Note: home office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit (thus reduces income tax and self-employment tax). But it cannot create or enlarge a net loss from that business.
- Recommendation: If you can substantiate rent and utility bills and you want the larger deduction, use the actual method (Form 8829). If you prefer simplicity and lower audit exposure, simplified is fine — but simplified gives a much smaller deduction in your case.

4) Forms you will use (federal)
- Form 1040 — U.S. Individual Income Tax Return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — Additional Income and Adjustments (for any adjustments like student loan interest if eligible; half of SE tax is reported as an adjustment).
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — Profit or Loss from Business (report your consulting income and business expenses, including home office deduction if using simplified; if using actual, show home office on Form 8829 and report the allowable portion on Schedule C).
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the actual method).
- Schedule SE — Self-Employment Tax (computes your SE tax; half of SE tax is an adjustment on Schedule 1).
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — Qualified Business Income deduction (QBI/Section 199A). If you have QBI from your Schedule C you may be eligible for a 20% deduction using Form 8995 (most small will use the simpler Form 8995). QBI has thresholds, but with your numbers you'd likely use Form 8995.
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions (only if you decide to itemize; from what you reported, unlikely beneficial).
- State: Arizona Form 140 (or appropriate AZ return) for your Arizona state income tax. State rules differ; some state returns require addbacks of certain adjustments.

5) Quick estimated calculations and conclusions (illustrative)
Using the facts you gave, here are rough calculations (rounded) to demonstrate phases/limits and help decide student loan interest and itemizing:

Option A — simplified home office ($775):
- Self-employment net profit ≈ 15,000 - 1,350 (business expenses) - 775 (home office simplified) = 12,875.
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × 12,875 ≈ $1,819 → half deductible ≈ $910.
- AGI ≈ 82,350 (W-2) + 1,370 (interest) + 12,875 (Schedule C net) - 910 (half SE tax) ≈ $95,685.
- AGI > $90,000 → student loan interest deduction likely phased out (not allowed).
- Itemized deductions (~$800) < standard $20,800 → take standard deduction.

Option B — actual home office (rent share ≈ $4,887):
- Self-employment net profit ≈ 15,000 - 1,350 - 4,887 = 8,763.
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × 8,763 ≈ $1,238 → half deductible ≈ $619.
- AGI ≈ 82,350 + 1,370 + 8,763 - 619 ≈ $91,864.
- Still slightly above $90,000 → likely student loan interest still phased out.
- Actual method gives bigger business deduction (lowers taxable income more), but still not enough to bring you under the $90k MAGI threshold in this scenario.

Important: these are estimates to illustrate likely results. Small differences (other deductions, retirement contributions, or different allocation of expenses) could change whether the student loan deduction is allowed. If you make a retirement plan contribution (Traditional IRA or SEP-IRA) that reduces MAGI, it might change eligibility.

6) Other considerations and recommendations
- Keep documentation: receipts for charitable gifts, bank interest statements (1099-INT), rent receipts, utility bills, and records tying the 155 sq ft to business use. For charitable, keep the $800 receipt.
- Estimated tax payments: because you have self‑employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes if you expect to owe $1,000+ in tax after withholdings. Check whether your W‑2 withholding covers the additional tax liability; otherwise, make estimated payments (Form 1040-ES) to avoid penalties.
- Retirement savings: funding a SEP‑IRA or Solo 401(k) can reduce taxable income and lower AGI for the year, which might help with MAGI-sensitive deductions. If you’re interested, I can show approximate contribution room and effects on AGI.
- State return: Arizona tax filing rules differ; the state may allow/differ on deductions. Don’t forget state returns.

7) Next steps / clarifying questions
- Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” expenses for your consulting (self‑employment) work? If not (i.e., they’re unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job), they’re not deductible on the federal return.
- Do you have any other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, property taxes, large state tax payments, large medical expenses)?
- Would you like me to run more precise numbers (including estimated federal tax, self‑employment tax, and whether a SEP-IRA or Traditional IRA contribution would allow you to reclaim the student loan interest deduction)?
- Do you want step-by-step help filling out any of the forms (I can prepare the text you’d put on Schedule C, Form 8829, etc.)?

If you want, I can (A) walk through a tighter numerical calculation using whichever home-office method you prefer, (B) show the forms/lines to fill in, or (C) list the records you should keep if you pick the actual home-office method.

