Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer up front
- You will almost certainly take the 2023 head‑of‑household standard deduction ($20,800) instead of itemizing based on the items you listed.  
- Your 2 consulting clients’ income is reported on Schedule C (profit/loss) and you can claim a home‑office deduction (either simplified or actual) against that self‑employment income.  
- You must file Form 1040 plus Schedule C and Schedule SE for the consulting income; use Form 8829 if you choose the actual home‑office method.  
- The student‑loan interest “deduction” is only allowed for interest paid in 2023 and phases out for single/HOH filers with MAGI $75,000–$90,000; with the incomes you gave you are likely above the phaseout and probably cannot take that deduction.  
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job are not deductible for 2023. If the $1,350 were business expenses of the consulting work, they belong on Schedule C.

Details and how I reached these conclusions

1) Income summary (based on your numbers)
- W‑2 wages: $82,350  
- Consulting (self‑employment): $15,000  
- Interest income: $890 (Citi) + $480 (Fidelity) = $1,370  
- Total gross income ≈ $98,720 (before any business or above‑the‑line adjustments).

2) Home office (self‑employment only)
Only self‑employed taxpayers (Schedule C) may claim the home‑office deduction. W‑2 employees cannot deduct a home office for federal purposes (except in very rare categories).

You have two methods:

- Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775. Report on Schedule C (no Form 8829).
- Actual expenses method: allocate a percentage of actual home expenses to the business.
  - Business percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%.
  - Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600. Rent allocable to business ≈ $24,600 × 19.87% ≈ $4,888.
  - If you have utilities, renter’s insurance portion, etc., those add to the deductible amount. Use Form 8829 to compute the allowable business use of home deduction when using actual expenses.

Effect on self‑employment income and self‑employment tax:
- Example (actual method, only rent allocated): Schedule C net profit ≈ $15,000 − $4,888 = $10,112.
- Self‑employment tax is computed on roughly 92.35% of that net profit; you then deduct one‑half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1). That reduces AGI.

Which method to choose:
- The actual method here (just rent allocated) gives a larger deduction (~$4,888) than the simplified ($775). If you have no other qualifying direct home expenses, the actual method likely yields a bigger tax benefit. But actual requires Form 8829 and proper records; simplified is easier. Compare both when preparing your return.

3) Student loan interest ($2,700)
Important clarifications:
- The student loan interest deduction is for interest you paid in the TAX YEAR (interest you paid in 2023). You can only deduct interest actually paid in 2023 and supported by Form 1098‑E from your servicer.
- The deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment up to $2,500 but phases out for single/HOH filers with MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000. Above $90,000 the deduction is not allowed.

With the income numbers above (even after typical business adjustments), your MAGI is likely at or above the $90,000 ceiling, so you probably cannot take the student‑loan interest deduction for 2023 unless you can materially reduce MAGI (see planning points below). Also confirm that the $2,700 you quoted was interest paid in 2023 — if it was paid in earlier years you cannot deduct it on your 2023 return.

4) Charitable contribution ($800)
- Cash gift to a qualified charity is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). There is no special universal above‑the‑line charitable deduction for 2023. Keep the receipt; you’ll need it if you itemize.
- $800 of cash gifts alone is unlikely to push you above the HOH standard deduction of $20,800, unless you have other itemizable items (mortgage interest, substantial state taxes paid, qualifying medical expenses, etc.).

5) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job — not deductible for 2023 (suspended by TCJA).
- If these are business expenses for the consulting work, list them on Schedule C (they reduce self‑employment income and SE tax).

6) Standard deduction vs. itemize
- 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household = $20,800.
- To itemize, your Schedule A total must exceed $20,800. Based on what you told me (only $800 charitable and no mortgage interest reported), you should take the standard deduction.
- If you paid significant state/local taxes (property tax or state income tax), mortgage interest, or large medical expenses >7.5% of AGI, re-evaluate itemizing. SALT deduction is capped at $10,000.

7) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).  
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments (e.g., half of self‑employment tax and, if allowed, student loan interest).  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report consulting income and business expenses (home office, unreimbursed business expenses related to consulting, etc.).  
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.  
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual‑expense method for your home‑office deduction (not required for simplified method).  
- Schedule A (Form 1040) — only if you choose to itemize (for charity receipt).  
- 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC (income reporting forms you should have received from clients) and 1099‑INT forms for the bank/broker interest (report those amounts on 1040).  
- Keep Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) if you paid interest in 2023 and plan to claim it.

Arizona state return
- You’ll also file Arizona’s individual return (AZ Form 140 or the correct 2023 AZ form). Arizona follows its own rules; you’ll report the federal AGI as the starting point and make AZ adjustments. If you want, I can help with state specifics once we finish federal.

Other important notes / planning ideas
- If you want to try to qualify for the student loan interest deduction, you would need to reduce your MAGI below the $90k threshold. Possible ways (if eligible) include making deductible retirement contributions that reduce AGI (SEP‑IRA or solo 401(k) contributions for the self‑employment income may be available). Eligibility and allowable amounts depend on facts (whether you are covered by an employer plan, amount of self‑employment net income, etc.). This is something we could model if you want.  
- Because you have self‑employment income, you owe SE tax and may need to have made estimated tax payments during 2023. If you underpaid, you may owe penalties. Check your W‑2 withholding and any estimated tax payments.  
- Keep documentation: receipts for charitable giving, records for home‑office expenses, invoices/receipts for consulting expenses, and Form 1098‑E if you paid student loan interest in 2023.

What I need from you to give a precise recommendation / estimate
- Did you actually pay $2,700 of student‑loan interest in 2023 (and do you have Form 1098‑E from MOHELA for 2023)? Or is that interest paid in earlier years?  
- Are the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses related to the consulting (self‑employment) or to your W‑2 job? Give a brief list of those expenses.  
- Do you have any mortgage interest, property tax, or significant state income tax paid in 2023 (or other itemizable items) beyond the $800 charity? Approximate amounts are fine.  
- Did you receive any 1099‑NEC for the consulting? Were any business expenses already reimbursed?  
- Are you covered by a retirement plan at Midwest Health Systems (employer plan)? (This affects IRA deductibility.)

If you want, I can:
- Run a quick estimated tax computation for 2023 using either the simplified or actual home‑office method and show estimated federal tax owed/refund.  
- Compare itemizing vs standard with any additional itemizable amounts you provide.  
- Help prepare the exact list of forms and a checklist to assemble before filing.

Which of the clarifying questions above can you answer now?

