Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and what federal/state forms you’ll need. A couple quick clarifying questions before final recommendations and a more exact tax estimate:

- Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” related to your consulting work (self-employment) or to your W‑2 job? (If they’re business expenses for your consulting, they’re deductible on Schedule C; if they’re unreimbursed employee expenses for the W‑2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025.)
- Do you rent (your $2,050/mo looks like rent) and does that rent include utilities, or do you pay utilities separately? (That matters for the “actual expense” home office calculation.)
- Did you receive any 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC / 1099‑K for the consulting income, or only payments with no 1099?

Summary (quick answers)
- Most likely you should take the 2023 Head‑of‑Household standard deduction ($20,800) rather than itemize (your itemizable amounts shown are small).
- You must report your consulting income on Schedule C and pay self‑employment tax via Schedule SE; you can deduct legitimate business expenses (and the home‑office share) against that income.
- Home‑office deduction: you can choose simplified ($5/sq ft) or the regular (actual expenses allocated by % of home). With your rent level, the actual method will probably give a larger deduction, but it requires Form 8829 and more recordkeeping.
- Student loan interest: the maximum deduction is $2,500, but it phases out for single/HOH filers with MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000. Given your gross income (~$98.7k) you may be phased out (or get only a partial deduction) — the exact determination depends on your MAGI after allowable adjustments (half of SE tax, retirement contributions, etc.).
- Charitable: your $800 to St. Mary’s is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Since the standard deduction for HOH is $20,800, $800 of cash giving alone is not enough to make itemizing worthwhile.
- Required federal forms: Form 1040; Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments); Schedule C (profit/loss from self‑employment); Schedule SE (self‑employment tax); Form 8829 if you use the regular home‑office method; Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) for Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction if you qualify; keep Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) and 1099‑INTs (Citi & Fidelity) for records (you don’t attach 1098‑E to return but you should have it).
- Arizona: file Arizona resident return (Form 140) and follow state rules; state forms may accept the federal AGI as starting point.

Detailed explanation and calculations (estimates)

1) Income you must report
- W‑2 wages: $82,350
- Consulting (self‑employment): $15,000 (report on Schedule C)
- Interest income: $890 (Citi) + $480 (Fidelity) = $1,370 (reported on Schedule 1 / 1040)

Total gross income ≈ $98,720.

2) Self‑employment and business deductions
- You must file Schedule C for the consulting income. Deductible business expenses reduce Schedule C profit and lower income subject to income tax and self‑employment (SE) tax.
- If your $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business-related (consulting), they belong on Schedule C.
- Home‑office deduction (see next section) also reduces Schedule C profit.

3) Home office (you said 155 sq ft exclusive; home is 780 sq ft)
- Business use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87% (≈19.9%).
- Two methods:

  a) Simplified method: $5 × 155 sq ft = $775. You do NOT file Form 8829; you report the $775 on Schedule C.

  b) Regular (actual-expense) method: multiply actual home expenses by business percent. With rent $2,050/mo = $24,600/yr:
     - Rent portion allocable to business = $24,600 × 19.87% ≈ $4,887.
     - If you also have utilities, renter’s insurance or other household costs, allocate 19.87% of each too and add them.
     - To use the regular method you complete Form 8829 and attach calculations to Schedule C.
- Given your rent, the regular method’s rent-only allocation (~$4,887) is substantially larger than the simplified $775. So the regular method will likely give a larger deduction — but it requires more recordkeeping and Form 8829.

4) Self‑employment tax and related deduction
- After calculating net Schedule C profit, you compute SE tax on net earnings (net profit × 0.9235 × 15.3%).
- You can deduct one‑half of SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (this reduces AGI).

Example (illustrative; assumes the $1,350 is a business expense and you use the regular home‑office allocation of rent only)
- Business expenses: $1,350 + home-office rent portion $4,887 = $6,237.
- Net Schedule C profit = $15,000 − $6,237 = $8,763.
- Net earnings for SE tax = $8,763 × 0.9235 ≈ $8,095.
- SE tax ≈ $8,095 × 15.3% ≈ $1,239. Half SE tax deduction ≈ $619.
- AGI before student interest adjustment = W‑2 $82,350 + interest $1,370 + net SE income $8,763 = $92,483; subtract half SE tax ≈ $619 → AGI ≈ $91,864 (approx).
- This AGI is above the $90,000 phaseout threshold for student loan interest for single/HOH filers, so you’d likely be in (or nearly in) the phaseout and may not be able to deduct the $2,700. The exact allowable amount (if any) depends on final MAGI calculation.

If instead:
- The $1,350 is not business‑related (i.e., not deductible), and you use the simplified home office ($775):
  - Net Schedule C profit = $15,000 − $775 = $14,225.
  - SE tax larger; half SE deduction increases little relative to the prior example, and AGI will be higher — making student loan interest deduction even more likely to be phased out.

5) Charitable contributions and itemizing
- Your deductible charitable cash gift is $800. To deduct it you must itemize (Schedule A). Unless you have other large itemizable items (state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or other charitable giving) your itemized total will be well below the HOH standard deduction of $20,800, so you should take the standard deduction.

6) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- You may be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction on qualified business income from the Schedule C activity. With modest net Schedule C profit (as in the example), a QBI deduction can be available and reduce taxable income further. Typically you’d complete Form 8995 (simple version) if your taxable income is below the threshold amounts. This is a post‑AGI deduction that reduces your taxable income.

7) Student loan interest
- Maximum $2,500 deduction (adjustment on Schedule 1) but phases out for single/HOH filers with MAGI between $75k and $90k. With estimated AGI above $90k you likely cannot claim the deduction; however, because MAGI is computed after certain adjustments, a precise calculation is needed when preparing the return. Keep Form 1098‑E from MOHELA.

8) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (additional income & adjustments) — interest and adjustments such as student loan interest (if allowed)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report consulting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method for home office
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) for QBI deduction if applicable
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you choose to itemize (doesn’t look likely here)
- Keep records/Forms: Form W‑2, 1099‑INTs from Citi & Fidelity, Form 1098‑E (student loan interest), any 1099‑NEC/1099‑K you received for consulting

Arizona state forms
- Arizona resident income tax return (Form 140 for 2023). Arizona generally starts with federal AGI, so many federal items flow to AZ. Check Arizona specifics for deductions/credits.

What I recommend you do next
1. Confirm whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are for your consulting work (business) or for your W‑2 job.
2. Gather documentation: 1099s, 1098‑E, 1099‑INT, receipts for the charitable donation, receipts/invoices for business expenses, rent statements (and utility bills if you’ll use actual method), and a short diagram or notes showing how you calculated the 155 sq ft exclusive business space.
