- You can deduct one‑half of SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (this reduces AGI).

Example (illustrative; assumes the $1,350 is a business expense and you use the regular home‑office allocation of rent only)
- Business expenses: $1,350 + home-office rent portion $4,887 = $6,237.
- Net Schedule C profit = $15,000 − $6,237 = $8,763.
- Net earnings for SE tax = $8,763 × 0.9235 ≈ $8,095.
- SE tax ≈ $8,095 × 15.3% ≈ $1,239. Half SE tax deduction ≈ $619.
- AGI before student interest adjustment = W‑2 $82,350 + interest $1,370 + net SE income $8,763 = $92,483; subtract half SE tax ≈ $619 → AGI ≈ $91,864 (approx).
- This AGI is above the $90,000 phaseout threshold for student loan interest for single/HOH filers, so you’d likely be in (or nearly in) the phaseout and may not be able to deduct the $2,700. The exact allowable amount (if any) depends on final MAGI calculation.

If instead:
- The $1,350 is not business‑related (i.e., not deductible), and you use the simplified home office ($775):
  - Net Schedule C profit = $15,000 − $775 = $14,225.
  - SE tax larger; half SE deduction increases little relative to the prior example, and AGI will be higher — making student loan interest deduction even more likely to be phased out.

5) Charitable contributions and itemizing
- Your deductible charitable cash gift is $800. To deduct it you must itemize (Schedule A). Unless you have other large itemizable items (state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or other charitable giving) your itemized total will be well below the HOH standard deduction of $20,800, so you should take the standard deduction.

6) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- You may be eligible for a 20% QBI deduction on qualified business income from the Schedule C activity. With modest net Schedule C profit (as in the example), a QBI deduction can be available and reduce taxable income further. Typically you’d complete Form 8995 (simple version) if your taxable income is below the threshold amounts. This is a post‑AGI deduction that reduces your taxable income.

7) Student loan interest
- Maximum $2,500 deduction (adjustment on Schedule 1) but phases out for single/HOH filers with MAGI between $75k and $90k. With estimated AGI above $90k you likely cannot claim the deduction; however, because MAGI is computed after certain adjustments, a precise calculation is needed when preparing the return. Keep Form 1098‑E from MOHELA.

8) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (additional income & adjustments) — interest and adjustments such as student loan interest (if allowed)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report consulting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method for home office
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) for QBI deduction if applicable
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you choose to itemize (doesn’t look likely here)
- Keep records/Forms: Form W‑2, 1099‑INTs from Citi & Fidelity, Form 1098‑E (student loan interest), any 1099‑NEC/1099‑K you received for consulting

Arizona state forms
- Arizona resident income tax return (Form 140 for 2023). Arizona generally starts with federal AGI, so many federal items flow to AZ. Check Arizona specifics for deductions/credits.

What I recommend you do next
1. Confirm whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are for your consulting work (business) or for your W‑2 job.
2. Gather documentation: 1099s, 1098‑E, 1099‑INT, receipts for the charitable donation, receipts/invoices for business expenses, rent statements (and utility bills if you’ll use actual method), and a short diagram or notes showing how you calculated the 155 sq ft exclusive business space.
3. If you’d like, I can:
   - Run a more exact tax estimate (federal + AZ) under both options (simplified vs. actual home‑office, business expense treated vs not) — I’ll need confirmation about the $1,350 and whether rent includes utilities.
   - Prepare a checklist of the exact numbers and boxes to enter on each form for you or for your tax preparer.

If you confirm the two quick clarifying points (business vs employee for the $1,350 and whether rent includes utilities), I’ll give a tighter, step‑by‑step estimate and concrete recommendation (simplified vs. actual home‑office) and show the likely student loan interest outcome.

