Thanks — I can walk you through this. A few quick clarifying questions before I give final, tailored numbers and the exact forms you’ll need:
1. Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” expenses related to your consulting work (self‑employment) or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job?
2. The $2,700 student‑loan interest — was that interest paid in tax year 2023, or are those amounts from earlier years (2016–2019)?

I’ll assume for the rest of this message that (A) the $1,350 are business expenses for the consulting work and (B) you rent your home (you stated $2,050/month), and I’ll show examples for both the simplified and actual home‑office methods. If either assumption is wrong I’ll update the calculations after your answers.

Short answers (summary)
- Likely better to take the 2023 head‑of‑household standard deduction ($20,800) rather than itemize, unless you have additional large itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, or medical expenses over 7.5% of AGI).
- Home office: you can deduct a home‑office deduction only for the consulting (self‑employment) activity, not for your W‑2 job. Because your home office is 155 sq ft of a 780 sq ft home (≈19.9%) and your rent is $2,050/month, the actual method will likely give a larger deduction than the simplified method. The home office deduction is claimed on Schedule C (actual method uses Form 8829).
- Student loan interest: the deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment (up to $2,500) but phases out for single/HOH taxpayers with modified AGI between $75,000 and $90,000 (2023). With the income numbers you gave, your MAGI/AGI will likely exceed $90,000, so you probably cannot take that deduction — but I’ll confirm if your $2,700 figure is for 2023 or earlier.
- Interest income: report the $890 (Citi) and $480 (Fidelity) on your 1040. Because total interest ($1,370) is under $1,500, you will not need Schedule B.
- Forms you will likely need: Form 1040; Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments) if applicable; Schedule C (profit/loss from business); Schedule SE (self‑employment tax); Form 8829 (if you use the actual home‑office method); Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) for the QBI deduction if applicable; Schedule A (only if you itemize). Also file Arizona Form 140 for state taxes.

Illustrative math (based on assumptions above)
Income:
- W‑2 wages: $82,350
- Self‑employment (consulting) gross: $15,000
- Interest: $890 + $480 = $1,370
Total gross income ≈ $98,720

Home office percentage:
- 155 / 780 = 0.1987 → ≈19.9%

Home office deduction examples:
1) Simplified method: $5 × 155 sq ft = $775 deduction (direct on Schedule C).
2) Actual method (rent only example): Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600. Home office share ≈ 24,600 × 0.1987 ≈ $4,889. (If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., a share of those is also deductible; if you own the home there’s depreciation to consider.)

Business expenses:
- If the $1,350 is business‑related, include it on Schedule C.
- Example using actual home office: business expenses = $4,889 (home share of rent) + $1,350 = $6,239.
- Net self‑employment income = $15,000 − $6,239 = $8,761.

Self‑employment tax:
- SE tax applies to 92.35% of net SE income: 0.9235 × 8,761 ≈ 8,089.
- SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 8,089 ≈ $1,237.
- Half of SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction ≈ $618 (reduces AGI).

AGI (approximate with actual home office):
- Gross 98,720 − half SE tax 618 = ≈ $98,102 (before any student loan interest deduction).
- Because this AGI ≈ $98k (> $90k), the student loan interest deduction phases out and likely is not allowed.

If you instead use the simplified home‑office method:
- Business expenses = $775 + $1,350 = $2,125.
- Net SE income = 15,000 − 2,125 = 12,875.
- SE tax ≈ 0.153 × (0.9235 × 12,875) ≈ $1,818; half ≈ $909.
- AGI ≈ 98,720 − 909 = 97,811 → still above $90k, so student loan interest likely disallowed.

Itemize vs standard
- Your only listed itemizable item is an $800 charitable cash donation. Even adding that to other typical deductions (SALT, mortgage interest) you’d need itemizable deductions > $20,800 to beat the head‑of‑household standard deduction.
- If you don’t have mortgage interest, large property taxes, or other big deductible items, take the standard deduction.

Other notes
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job are not deductible for federal returns (suspended by TCJA), so they only help if they are business expenses for your consulting work.
- Keep receipts: you’ll need documentation for the $800 donation, business expenses, and home office (plan/diagram showing exclusive area, photos, receipts for rent/utilities if using actual method).
- QBI deduction: you may be eligible for the 20% Qualified Business Income deduction on the net Schedule C profit; because your taxable income is well under the QBI thresholds for 2023, you likely can take it. Form 8995 is used for the simplified QBI deduction. It’s computed after your net profit & before certain adjustments — I can estimate that after you confirm the $1,350 and student loan timing.
- Estimated tax/withholding: because you are self‑employed for part of the year, you may owe self‑employment tax and possibly quarterly estimated payments. Check whether your W‑2 withholding covers the additional tax; if not, consider making estimated payments.

Forms you will likely use (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 — additional income/adjustments (reports business income from Schedule C, adjustments such as half SE tax if applicable).
- Schedule C — report consulting income and business expenses (including the home office deduction if using simplified or actual; if simplified you put the $ on Schedule C, if actual use Form 8829).
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (use only if taking the actual method).
- Schedule SE — self‑employment tax.
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — QBI deduction (likely Form 8995 for your income level).
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (doesn’t seem likely given the data).
- Arizona Form 140 (and any AZ schedules) — your state return.

What I recommend next
1. Tell me whether the $1,350 expenses are for consulting or your W‑2 job, and whether the $2,700 student‑loan interest was paid in 2023 (or earlier).
2. If you want, I’ll run a precise calculation (AGI, taxable income, estimated federal tax due, SE tax, and whether QBI applies) under both simplified and actual home‑office methods so you can choose the better method and see if you should make estimated payments.

If you prefer, I can also produce a checklist of documents and a step‑by‑step plan to prepare the return or for taking to a tax preparer.

