Below I’ll walk through how your 2023 tax picture looks, show rough numbers, explain what’s deductible, whether to itemize, how to handle the home office, and list the federal (and state) forms you’ll likely need. I’ll also flag items to double‑check and actions you can take if you want to lower your taxable income.

Quick summary / bottom line
- You should file Form 1040 with Schedule C and Schedule SE for the consulting income.
- The standard deduction for Head of Household (2023) is $20,800 — that will almost certainly be larger than your itemized deductions, so you will very likely take the standard deduction.
- Your student loan interest deduction is limited to $2,500 and phases out at higher MAGI (for single/HOH: $75,000–$90,000). Based on the numbers you gave, your MAGI is likely above $90,000, so you probably cannot claim the student loan interest deduction (but check exact MAGI after SE adjustments).
- Home-office: because you’re self‑employed, you can take a home‑office deduction. You can choose the simplified method ($5 × eligible sq ft = $775) or the regular method (allocate actual rent/utilities by percentage — appears to be ~19.9% of rent = about $4,900). The regular method will likely give a larger deduction here; you’ll report it on Schedule C (Form 8829 is used if you use the regular method).

Detailed calculations and reasoning (estimates)
Income
- W‑2 wages: $82,350
- Consulting (self‑employment) gross: $15,000
- Interest: $890 (Citi) + $480 (Fidelity) = $1,370
Estimated gross income = 82,350 + 15,000 + 1,370 = $98,720

Self‑employment adjustments
- You reported $1,350 of unreimbursed expenses for the consulting. Those are deductible on Schedule C, reducing self‑employment net to 15,000 − 1,350 = $13,650.
- Self‑employment tax: roughly 15.3% on 92.35% of net SE income. For net ≈ $13,650 SE tax ≈ $1,930 and half of that (≈ $965) is an above‑the‑line deduction (reduces AGI).

Student loan interest deduction
- Maximum deduction = $2,500 (for 2023).
- Phaseout for single/head of household: MAGI $75,000 → $90,000. If your MAGI is $90,000 or above, you cannot claim the deduction.
- Rough check: your income before student loan interest and after the half‑SE tax adjustment is still likely above $90k (82,350 + 13,650 + 1,370 − ~965 ≈ $96,405), so the student loan interest deduction will likely be fully phased out for you. You should calculate MAGI precisely (there are specific adjustments that affect MAGI) — but based on the numbers you gave, you likely cannot claim the $2,700.

Charitable contribution
- $800 cash gift to St. Mary’s Food Bank is an itemized deduction (if you itemize). You have a receipt, which is good substantiation.
- Note: charitable gifts are only beneficial if your total itemized deductions exceed the standard deduction.

Itemize vs standard deduction
- Standard deduction (Head of Household, 2023) = $20,800.
- Your known itemized deductions: $800 charitable. You did not list mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or significant state/local taxes beyond standard payroll/state withholding on the W‑2. Unless you have substantial additional itemizable items (large state tax payments, mortgage interest, unreimbursed casualty losses, large medical expenses, etc.), your itemized total will be far below $20,800.
- Recommendation: take the standard deduction for 2023 unless you discover other itemizable amounts that push you above $20,800.

Home office deduction (self‑employed)
You said: exclusive use, 155 sq ft exclusively, total home 780 sq ft, rent $2,050/month.
- Business use percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87% (~19.9%).
- Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600.
- Regular method (allocate actual expenses): rent portion deductible ≈ 24,600 × 19.87% ≈ $4,889. If you have separately identifiable utilities/insurance/etc., add your business share of those too. Because you rent, you don’t take depreciation (depreciation applies when you own the home).
- Simplified method: $5 × 155 sq ft = $775.
- Because the regular method deduction of about $4,889 is less than your net self‑employment income (~$13,650), you can use the regular method and claim that deduction (subject to documentation/exclusive‑use rules). The regular method is likely more beneficial here.
- The home‑office deduction is reported on Schedule C. If you use the regular method, use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to compute and support the deduction; simplified method is reported directly on Schedule C.

Other tax consequences for the consulting income
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) on your net consulting income (~$1,930 approximate). Half of that is deductible (above) and reduces your income tax.
- Make sure you report the gross consulting receipts even if you did not receive 1099‑NEC forms; pay attention to any 1099s you received and reconcile to your income.

Interest reporting
- Your total interest is $1,370. Schedule B is required only if taxable interest is more than $1,500 — so you probably don’t need Schedule B.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual income tax return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — for adjustments (half of SE tax, student loan interest if allowed, etc.)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report consulting income and business expenses (the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses belong here)
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and the deductible half
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the regular method to calculate the home‑office deduction; if you use simplified method, you do not file 8829
- Possibly Schedule A only if you decide to itemize (doesn’t look likely)
- State return: Arizona Form 140 (AZ individual income tax return) — you must file an AZ return; state rules largely follow federal treatment of most items but check state specifics (AZ has its own standard deduction/credits).

Documentation to keep
- Receipts for charitable donation(s) (you have the receipt) — keep it.
- Records supporting consulting income and business expenses (invoices, bank deposits, receipts for supplies, cell/Internet allocation if claimed, etc.).
- Lease and rent payment records (to substantiate the rent and home office percentage).
- Records of student loan interest paid (Form 1098‑E etc.) — even if you are phased out, keep the records.

Optional planning suggestions (if you want to lower taxable income)
- If you want to try to reclaim some student loan interest deduction, adding pre‑tax retirement contributions (SEP IRA or solo 401(k)) could reduce your MAGI. SEP IRA contributions are deductible as a business expense on Schedule C and reduce AGI. You’d need to evaluate amounts and deadlines. A traditional IRA contribution might be deductible depending on your participation in an employer plan and income limits. Consult a tax preparer before making moves.
- Keep good records and separate personal vs business expenses going forward.

Next steps I can help with
- If you want, I can:
  - Draft a checklist you can use to gather documents for filing.
  - Run more precise numbers if you provide year‑end W‑2 with federal/state withholding, exact amounts of any other itemizable items (state taxes paid, medical expenses, mortgage interest if any), and exact business expense categories (utilities, internet portion, supplies).
  - Prepare a draft Schedule C calculation (estimate) showing both simplified and regular home‑office methods so you can compare net profit and taxes.

Would you like me to (A) produce a printable checklist and a detailed Schedule C/SE calculation using the regular home‑office allocation, or (B) compute whether a SEP IRA contribution could lower your MAGI enough to claim any student loan interest deduction?

