Short answers up front
- Take the standard deduction (Head of Household standard deduction for 2023 = $20,800). With only $800 of charitable donations and no mortgage interest/property tax shown, your likely itemized total is far less than $20,800.
- You can — and should — deduct your self‑employment business expenses (the $1,350 if those are consulting expenses) and a home‑office deduction for the consulting work. Those reduce your business net income (and AGI) whether or not you itemize.
- Student loan interest: you reported $2,700 paid, but the 2023 maximum deduction is $2,500 and it phases out for single/HOH filers with MAGI $75,000–$90,000. Based on the numbers you gave, your MAGI is above $90k, so you likely cannot take any student‑loan interest deduction for 2023.
- You will owe self‑employment tax on the consulting net income and must file Schedule C and Schedule SE. Use Form 8829 if you claim the home office using the actual‑expense method.

Detailed breakdown and math (estimates)
1) Income summary (what you reported)
- W‑2 wages: $82,350
- Consulting: $15,000
- Interest income: $890 (Citi) + $480 (Fidelity) = $1,370
Total gross before business expense adjustments = $98,720.

2) Business (consulting) expenses and self‑employment tax
- If the $1,350 you listed are business expenses for the consulting work, your net profit = $15,000 − $1,350 = $13,650 (reported on Schedule C).
- Net earnings subject to self‑employment tax = 13,650 × 0.9235 ≈ $12,615.
- Self‑employment tax ≈ $12,615 × 15.3% ≈ $1,930. Half of that ($≈965) is an adjustment to income (reduces AGI).

3) Student loan interest
- You paid $2,700 to MOHELA for interest (2016–2019 loans). The maximum federal student‑loan interest deduction in 2023 is $2,500 if you qualify.
- Phaseout: for single/head‑of‑household filers, the deduction phases out for MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 and is fully phased out above $90,000.
- Estimated MAGI before student loan interest (using numbers above and including the half SE tax adjustment) is roughly $96,400, which is above $90k. Therefore you would not be able to claim the student‑loan interest deduction for 2023.
(Note: deductible IRA or other allowable adjustments could reduce MAGI; see “Options” below.)

4) Charitable contributions
- Your $800 cash donation to St. Mary’s Food Bank is an itemized charitable deduction (keep the receipt). There is no above‑the‑line universal charitable deduction for 2023, so it only helps if you itemize.
- Because the standard deduction for HOH ($20,800) is much larger than $800, itemizing only for this gift would not be beneficial.

5) Home‑office deduction
- You said the office is 155 sq ft exclusively used, home = 780 sq ft → business use percentage = 155/780 ≈ 19.87%.
- Rent: $2,050/month → $24,600/year. Business portion of rent ≈ $24,600 × 19.87% ≈ $4,888.
- Two ways to claim:
  a) Simplified method: $5 × 155 sq ft = $775 (easy, no Form 8829).
  b) Actual expenses: allocate your actual rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., by the 19.87% business percentage and use Form 8829. With rent alone, that would be ≈ $4,888 (plus a share of utilities if applicable), which is larger than the simplified amount.
- Important limits: home‑office deductions are taken on Schedule C (or on Form 8829 if using actual method) and cannot exceed your net profit from that business. Excess deductible home‑office amounts may be carried forward under the rules for Form 8829.

6) Unreimbursed employee expenses
- Unreimbursed expenses for a W‑2 job are generally NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 under current law (except for certain narrow categories). If your $1,350 are expenses for your consulting business, they are deductible on Schedule C; if they are expenses from your W‑2 employment, they are not deductible now.

7) Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- Likely take the standard deduction: head‑of‑household standard = $20,800 for 2023. With only $800 in charitable gifts and no mortgage interest/property tax or large medical expenses reported, itemizing will almost certainly be lower than $20,800.
- But important: even if you take the standard deduction you still report and deduct business expenses and the home office on Schedule C (these are above/before the standard vs itemize decision).

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments) — e.g., adjustments such as deductible part of SE tax will flow here
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report consulting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and the half‑SE tax deduction
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for the home office. If you use the simplified method you do not file Form 8829; report the simplified amount on Schedule C.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you opt to itemize (probably not needed here)
- Keep/Form(s) you should have: W‑2 from Midwest Health Systems, any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from consulting clients if issued, 1099‑INTs from Citi and Fidelity, receipts for charitable contribution.

State return (Arizona)
- You will file Arizona Form 140 (or other applicable AZ form). Arizona starts from your federal AGI but check for AZ‑specific adjustments. If you want, I can outline the AZ specifics when you’re ready.

Other considerations and suggestions
- You likely owe self‑employment tax (~$1,930 estimated) and additional income tax since your combined income is substantial; check whether you had enough withholding or estimated payments. You may need to make estimated payments for 2024.
- If you want to lower MAGI (and possibly reclaim student‑loan interest deduction), you could consider deductible contributions (traditional IRA if eligible) made by the tax‑filing deadline. Whether you’re eligible for a deductible IRA contribution depends on whether you (or a spouse) are covered by a workplace retirement plan and on your income. Consult your preparer for this planning — it can be complicated.
- Keep documentation: receipts for charitable gifts, home office (square footage documentation and utility/rent bills), business receipts, mileage log (if you use a vehicle for business), 1099s/1098s, and student loan interest statements (Form 1098‑E).

If you want, I can:
- Run a rough tax estimate showing taxable income, self‑employment tax, and likely federal tax owed/refund using the numbers you gave.
- Prepare a fillable checklist of items and amounts to give to a tax preparer.
- Help determine whether those $1,350 expenses are best treated as Schedule C business expenses (if you confirm they are for consulting).

Which would you like next?

