Short answer up front
- You should report: W-2 wages ($82,350), consulting income ($15,000) on Schedule C, and the interest ($890 + $480 = $1,370) on Form 1040 as interest income.
- Your home office deduction is available only for the self-employment (consulting) work. You can use the simplified method (155 sq ft × $5 = $775) or the actual-expense method (about 19.9% of rent/utilities/etc. because 155 ÷ 780 ≈ 19.87%).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for a W-2 job are NOT deductible for 2018–2025. If the $1,350 are ordinary and necessary business expenses for your consulting business, they are deductible on Schedule C.
- Student loan interest: the maximum adjustment is $2,500, but it phases out for single/head-of-household filers with MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 (2023). With your W‑2 and other income, you are likely at or above the phaseout threshold and may not be able to claim the full $2,500 (possibly none). See below for a quick estimate and how to confirm.
- Itemize vs standard: the 2023 standard deduction for head of household is $20,800. Your reported itemizable deduction so far (charity $800) is far below that, so you will almost certainly take the standard deduction on your federal return.
- Forms you’ll likely need: Form 1040, Schedule C (profit/loss from business), Schedule SE (self-employment tax), Schedule 1 (adjustments to income, e.g., half of SE tax and possibly student loan interest), Form 8829 (business use of home) if you use the actual-expense home-office method, Form 8995 (if you want to claim the Qualified Business Income 20% deduction), and Arizona resident return (AZ Form 140).

Detailed guidance and mini-calculations

1) Income to report
- W-2: $82,350 (reported on Form 1040 from your W-2).
- Consulting: $15,000 — report on Schedule C (gross receipts). Even if you didn’t receive 1099s, you must report it.
- Interest: $1,370 — report as interest income (1099‑INTs or statement).  

2) Business (consulting) expenses and the “unreimbursed expenses”
- If the $1,350 are business expenses related to your consulting (supplies, software, travel, marketing, etc.), claim them on Schedule C as ordinary and necessary business expenses.
- If those $1,350 are unreimbursed employee business expenses tied to your W‑2 job, they are not deductible under current law (2018–2025 suspension of miscellaneous itemized deductions).

3) Home office (you said 155 sq ft exclusive; home is 780 sq ft, rent $2,050/month)
- Eligibility: Home office deduction is only for business owners/self-employed individuals. (W-2 employees cannot take it for 2018–2025.)
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 × 155 sq ft = $775 deductible on Schedule C.
  b) Actual-expense method: allocate a percentage of allowable housing expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) to the business equal to business square footage ÷ total square footage. Your percentage ≈ 155 ÷ 780 ≈ 19.87%. Example using rent only: $2,050 × 12 = $24,600 annual rent × 19.87% ≈ $4,887 claimed. You would also add allocated utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. Using actual expenses requires Form 8829; simplified method does not.
- Which to choose? Compare the two. For your rent amount, actual method will likely give a larger deduction (assuming you have some utility/insurance amounts). But the simplified method is easier and may be preferable if you don’t want to track all home expenses or if actual expenses don’t exceed the simplified amount by much.

4) Self‑employment tax and related adjustments
- You’ll owe self-employment (SE) tax on net Schedule C profit. SE tax = 15.3% on 92.35% of net self-employed earnings. Half of SE tax is an above-the-line deduction on Form 1040 (reported on Schedule 1).
- Example rough numbers (to illustrate effect):
  - Scenario A (simplified home office + business expenses): Net consulting = 15,000 − 1,350 − 775 = 12,875. SE tax ≈ 15.3% × (12,875 × 0.9235) ≈ $1,820; half deductible ≈ $910.
  - Scenario B (actual home-office using rent only): Net consulting = 15,000 − 1,350 − 4,887 ≈ 8,763. SE tax ≈ 15.3% × (8,763 × 0.9235) ≈ $1,240; half deductible ≈ $620.
- The half-SE tax deduction reduces your AGI, which can affect phaseouts (student loan interest, etc.).

5) Student loan interest ($2,700 you paid)
- Maximum deduction is $2,500 for 2023 (even if you paid $2,700).
- Phaseout for Single/Head of Household in 2023: MAGI $75,000–$90,000. Above $90,000 → no deduction. (These are 2023 IRS thresholds.)
- With your W‑2 and other income, you are likely at or above the $90k phaseout ceiling before the student loan interest deduction, so you may not be able to claim it. If you want, I can run a precise MAGI calculation based on whether you take the actual or simplified home-office deduction and whether the $1,350 are business expenses.

6) Charitable giving
- $800 cash gift to St. Mary’s Food Bank is an itemized deduction (if you itemize). Because the standard deduction for HOH is $20,800, itemizing solely for $800 is not beneficial. Keep the receipt for recordkeeping.

7) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for head of household: $20,800. Your current itemizable amounts mentioned ($800 charity, no mortgage interest, etc.) are far below that. So you should take the standard deduction on the federal return unless you have other substantial itemizable deductions (state/local taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap, mortgage interest, big medical expenses above the AGI floor, sizable charitable/giving, etc.).
- Note: student loan interest is an adjustment to income (above-the-line), not an itemized deduction.

8) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- As a self-employed individual, you may be eligible for the 20% QBI deduction (Section 199A) on qualified business income from your consulting. For 2023 your taxable income is well below the 2023 thresholds (single/HOH threshold ~$182,100), so you likely qualify for up to 20% of your qualified net business income (after deductions). That deduction is taken after AGI calculation and may reduce taxable income further. It’s reported on Form 1040 (Form 8995 or 8995-A).

9) Forms you will use (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income: half of SE tax, possibly student loan interest if allowed).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) for consulting income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) only if you choose the actual-expense method for the home office.
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) for the Qualified Business Income deduction (if eligible).
- 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC receipts (if any), 1099‑INTs for interest, W-2, and 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) if provided by MOHELA — retain the statements/receipts.

10) State (Arizona)
- Arizona resident return (AZ Form 140) — you’ll report the same federal income; Arizona has its own standard/itemized rules and credits. Check available deductions on the AZ return. You’ll also report Arizona taxable interest/other income and possibly get a state tax liability.

11) Recordkeeping and other notes
- Keep receipts for the charitable donation, records for home office square footage and how you calculated the allocation, invoices/receipts for business expenses, 1098‑E or loan statements for student loan interest, and 1099‑INTs.
- If your consulting generates tax due beyond withholdings, consider quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid penalties (Form 1040-ES).
- If you want, I can estimate your federal tax and self-employment tax liability under the two home-office scenarios (simplified vs actual) and show whether you’d be able to claim any student loan interest. To do that I’ll need to know:
  - Are the $1,350 expenses business expenses for the consulting (or are they employee/W-2 expenses)?
  - Do you have any other deductible items (state taxes paid, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.)?
  - Do you have amounts for utilities, renter’s insurance, or other home expenses if you want to try the actual home-office method?

Would you like me to run the two sample calculations (simplified vs actual home-office) and show estimated AGI, taxable income, SE tax, and whether you qualify for the student loan interest deduction? If so, please confirm whether the $1,350 is business-related and list any other deductions/expenses you haven’t mentioned.

