Summary answer (short)
- Most likely you should take the 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household ($20,800) — your itemizable deductions (charity $800, no mortgage interest shown, SALT unknown) look much smaller than the standard deduction.
- Your consulting income (self‑employment) is reported on Schedule C and can take a home‑office deduction (only for the consulting work). That deduction reduces your self‑employment income (and thus your AGI) and is NOT part of Schedule A itemizing.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for the W‑2 job are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 unless they’re actually business expenses of your consulting activity.
- Student loan interest (up to $2,500) is an above‑the‑line deduction but phases out for MAGI between $75,000 and $90,000 (single/HOH). With the numbers you gave, your MAGI is very likely above $90k so the student‑loan deduction will be eliminated — but that depends on exact MAGI after self‑employment deductions and half of SE tax.
- Forms you’ll normally need: Form 1040, Schedule C (profit/loss from self‑employment), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Schedule 1 (for adjustments including student loan interest if allowed), Form 8829 (if you use the actual method for home office), and Schedule A only if you choose to itemize. Also Arizona form(s) to file the state return (AZ Form 140 for residents). You’ll also use 1099‑INTs, any 1099‑NEC, W‑2, and 1098‑E (student loan interest).

Detailed explanation, calculations, and next steps

1) Income recap (what you reported)
- W‑2 wages: $82,350
- Self‑employment/consulting: $15,000 (2 clients)
- Interest: $890 (Citi) + $480 (Fidelity) = $1,370
- Total gross before adjustments: $98,720

2) Home office (only for self‑employed consulting)
You can claim a business use‑of‑home deduction only for the part of your home used regularly and exclusively for the consulting business. You said: 155 sq ft exclusive; total 780 sq ft; rent $2,050/month.

- Business percentage = 155 / 780 = 19.87% (≈0.1987)
- Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600
- Actual method (allocate actual home expenses): home office share of rent = $24,600 × 19.87% ≈ $4,888
  - (If you use actual method you can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.; Form 8829 is used for the calculation.)
- Simplified method: $5.00 × 155 sq ft = $775 (you simply enter that on Schedule C; no Form 8829 needed)

Which to pick?
- Actual method usually gives a larger deduction here ($4,888 vs $775) because your rent is relatively high. Use actual if you can document rent, utilities, etc. (Form 8829 required for actual).
- Remember: home‑office deduction can only reduce your business profit. It cannot be used for your W‑2 job.

3) Self‑employment tax and impact on MAGI / student loan interest
Self‑employment income is subject to SE tax (~15.3% on net earnings). You get to deduct half of SE tax as an adjustment to income (reduces AGI/MAGI which affects the student loan interest phaseout).

Examples (rounded):
- If you use actual home‑office ($4,888) and have no other business deductions:
  - Net self‑employment income = $15,000 − $4,888 = $10,112
  - SE tax ≈ 15.3% × (10,112 × 0.9235) ≈ $1,430
  - Half SE tax deduction ≈ $715
  - Approx AGI ≈ 82,350 + 10,112 + 1,370 − 715 ≈ $93,117 → above $90k phaseout limit
- If you use simplified method ($775):
  - Net SE income ≈ $14,225; SE tax ≈ $2,011; half ≈ $1,006; AGI ≈ $97,940 → above limit
- If your $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are actually business expenses for the consulting work and you claim them on Schedule C, that further lowers SE income. Example with actual home office + $1,350 business expenses:
  - Net SE = 15,000 − 4,888 − 1,350 = 8,762 → AGI ≈ $91,863 → still likely above $90k

Conclusion about student loan interest (your $2,700)
- The student loan interest deduction is limited to $2,500 and phases out for MAGI $75k–$90k (single/HOH). With the figures you gave, MAGI is very likely above $90k so the deduction will probably be disallowed. It’s worth calculating precise MAGI after all Schedule C deductions and half of SE tax to be certain.
- You’ll need Form 1098‑E from MOHELA to substantiate interest paid in 2023. Note: only interest paid in 2023 qualifies.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If these are expenses of your consulting business (travel, supplies, software, etc.), deduct them on Schedule C.
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses related only to your W‑2 employer, they are not deductible for 2018–2025.

5) Charitable giving ($800)
- Deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. There is no “above‑the‑line” deduction for cash gifts for 2023 (the temporary $300/$600 provisions are gone). Because your standard deduction as HOH is $20,800, $800 in charitable gifts alone will not make itemizing worthwhile unless you have other substantial itemizable deductions (state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses above threshold, etc.).

6) Which deduction method will likely be best?
- Very likely take the standard deduction ($20,800) for head of household.
- Claim business expenses (including home office) on Schedule C (these reduce your taxable income even though you still take the standard deduction for personal itemization). Those business deductions are independent from your decision whether to itemize personal deductions.

7) Forms you will generally use for federal filing (2023)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — student loan interest deduction, etc., flows here if allowed
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report consulting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — use if you elect the actual expenses method for business use of home
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (for charity and other itemized deductions)
- Attach/keep: W‑2 from Midwest Health Systems, 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC if you received them from clients, 1099‑INTs from banks, 1098‑E for student loan interest
- Arizona: AZ individual income tax return (AZ Form 140 for residents) and any state schedules required

8) Other items and practical advice
- Estimated taxes: With self‑employment income you may owe self‑employment tax and additional income tax. If the clients did not withhold, you may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid penalties.
- Recordkeeping: keep receipts for donations, documentation for home office (floor plan, measurement, rent payments, utility bills), receipts for business expenses, and 1098‑E and 1099s.
- If you want to reduce MAGI for purposes of the student loan interest phaseout, consider retirement plan contributions available to the self‑employed (SEP IRA, solo 401(k)) — those reduce taxable income but timing and eligibility matter.
- If your situation is borderline or you want to maximize retirement and tax planning, consider consulting a CPA/tax preparer — especially because small differences in business expenses or retirement contributions can change whether you get the student‑loan deduction.

What I need from you to give precise numbers / next steps
- Were the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” for the consulting activity (self‑employment) or expenses tied to your W‑2 job?
- Did you actually pay $2,700 in student‑loan interest in 2023? Do you have Form 1098‑E from MOHELA for 2023?
- Did you receive any 1099‑NEC(s) from your consulting clients? Any other business expenses you haven’t listed (software, mileage, phone, supplies, etc.)?
- Do you rent (I assumed yes from monthly payment) and do you pay utilities that you’d like to allocate to the home‑office actual method?

If you want, I can:
- Run precise AGI/MAGI and tax‑owed estimates with different home‑office and business‑expense scenarios, or
- Draft a checklist and the forms you’ll need to gather to file, or
- Suggest how large a SEP/Solo‑401(k) contribution would need to be to bring your MAGI under $90k (if you want to try to reclaim the student‑loan interest deduction).

