User: I want to file my 2023 taxes correctly. I'm head of household in Phoenix AZ. W-2 from Midwest Health Systems: $82,350. Additional income: $15,000 (consulting, 2 clients), $890 from Citi savings, $480 from Fidelity. Home office: 155 sq ft exclusive; I pay $2,050/month for 780 sq ft. Deductions: $2,700 student loan interest (MOHELA, 2016–2019); $800 to St. Mary's Food Bank (have the receipt) with receipt; $1,350 unreimbursed expenses. Can you tell me what I can deduct, whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, and what forms I need?
Assistant: Short answer first
- You will report the $15,000 consulting as self‑employment income on Schedule C and pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). The home office deduction can be taken only against the self‑employment income (not against your W‑2 wages).
- You almost certainly should take the 2023 head‑of‑household standard deduction ($20,800) rather than itemizing based on the items you listed.
- The student‑loan interest deduction is limited by an income phase‑out; with the numbers you gave you are likely above the phaseout and therefore probably cannot claim it — but I need one clarification (below) to confirm.
- Forms you’ll use: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, possibly Form 8829 (if you use actual home‑office expenses), Schedule 1 (for adjustments), and Arizona Form 140 for your state return. See below for details and calculations.

Items-by-item (what’s deductible and how)

1) Consulting income ($15,000)
- Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses against that income on Schedule C (supplies, a proportion of rent/utilities for a valid home office, business mileage, phone/Internet portion, etc.).
- Net profit then flows to Form 1040; you’ll also owe self‑employment tax on net profit (reported on Schedule SE). You may deduct one-half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).

2) Home‑office (155 sq ft exclusive use; home 780 sq ft; rent $2,050/month)
- Because you are self‑employed for the consulting, you can take a home‑office deduction for the portion of your rent/utilities that corresponds to that office space, provided the home office is used regularly and exclusively for the business.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 x 155 sq ft = $775 (no Form 8829 needed).
  - Actual (regular) method: allocate actual home expenses by the business percentage = 155 / 780 ≈ 19.87%. Example using only rent:
    - Annual rent = $2,050 × 12 = $24,600
    - Business portion of rent ≈ 24,600 × 0.1987 ≈ $4,889
    - You may also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., by the same percentage. (As a renter you cannot depreciate the home itself.)
  - If you use the actual method and have depreciation (homeowner), or other qualifying actual expenses, you must complete Form 8829 and attach it. As a renter without depreciation, actual method still works for rent/utilities and may be larger than the simplified method.
- The home‑office deduction reduces Schedule C net profit (thus reduces income subject to income tax and SE tax).

3) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If these are business expenses related to your consulting clients, they are deductible on Schedule C (not the “miscellaneous employee” deduction). Combine them with other Schedule C expenses.
- If they are unreimbursed expenses related to your W‑2 job (employee expenses), they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspension under current law), except for very limited categories (not typical). So please confirm whether these $1,350 are business expenses for the consulting.

4) Charitable cash gift $800 to St. Mary’s Food Bank
- Cash gifts to qualified charities are deductible only if you itemize. For 2023 there is no special above‑the‑line deduction for non‑itemizers.
- You have a receipt — for cash gifts of $250 or more you need a written acknowledgement from the charity to substantiate the deduction (you said you have one, good).
- Because the 2023 standard deduction for Head of Household is $20,800, a single $800 charitable gift (plus likely small other itemizable items) will usually not push you past the standard deduction — so it’s unlikely useful to itemize just for the $800.

5) Interest from Citi ($890) and Fidelity ($480)
- Interest income totals $1,370. Report this on your Form 1040. You generally receive 1099‑INTs from the banks.
- You don’t need Schedule B unless total taxable interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 (so you do not need Schedule B with $1,370).

6) Student‑loan interest ($2,700)
- The student‑loan interest deduction (maximum $2,500) is an above‑the‑line adjustment to income but phases out based on Modified AGI.
- For 2023 the phaseout for single/head‑of‑household is MAGI $75,000–$90,000 (partial; above $90k you cannot take it). Based on the numbers you gave, you are likely over the $90,000 upper limit and therefore would not be allowed the student loan deduction for 2023 — unless some facts change (see clarifying Qs below).
- Important: you can only deduct interest actually paid in 2023 (you should have received Form 1098‑E from MOHELA if you paid qualifying interest in 2023). The $2,700 figure you quoted — please confirm that this $2,700 is interest you actually paid in 2023 (not payments from earlier years).

Illustrative estimate (to show why student loan deduction likely phases out)
- Quick example using actual home‑office allocation + unreimbursed business expenses (assuming those are business related):
  - Rent yearly = $24,600; home‑office portion ≈ $4,889
  - Business expenses = $1,350
  - Total Schedule C expenses ≈ $6,239 → Net Schedule C profit ≈ $15,000 − $6,239 = $8,761
  - Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × 8,761 ≈ $1,238; half SE tax ≈ $619
  - Total income included on Form 1040: W‑2 $82,350 + net self‑employment $8,761 + interest $1,370 = ≈ $92,481
  - AGI after half SE tax deduction ≈ $92,481 − $619 = ≈ $91,862
  - That is above the $90,000 MAGI limit for the student‑loan interest deduction for HOH, so likely you cannot claim it. (If your Schedule C expenses are higher, or if the $1,350 are not business expenses, the result changes; this is an estimate.)

Will you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- Likely take the standard deduction (Head of Household in 2023 = $20,800). Based on the items you listed (charity $800 and no mortgage interest or large property tax), your itemized deductions will probably be well below $20,800.
- Remember: home‑office and business expenses are not “itemized deductions” — they reduce self‑employment income on Schedule C (above the line), which reduces AGI regardless of whether you itemize or not.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — e.g., student loan interest if allowed, half SE tax goes on Schedule 1
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the $15,000 consulting and related expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual expense method for the home office (not needed for simplified method)
- Schedule B — only required if interest+dividends > $1,500 (not required for your amounts)
- Keep Form 1099‑NEC (if you receive one for consulting) and 1099‑INTs (banks), and Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) if applicable.

Arizona state return
- Arizona resident return (Form 140 or the correct AZ resident form) — Arizona generally begins with federal AGI and makes state adjustments. Check AZ instructions or ask if you want me to walk through the state return specifics.

Records to keep
- Receipts for the $800 charitable gift (written acknowledgement)
- Bank statements / 1099‑INTs for interest
- 1099‑NEC (if issued) or records of payments received from the two clients
- Documentation of home‑office measurement, rent statements, utilities paid, and how you calculated the percentage (exclusive-use requirement)
- Records/receipts for the $1,350 in business expenses
- Form 1098‑E or records showing student loan interest paid in 2023

Questions I need to confirm to finalize advice
1. Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the consulting clients (i.e., ordinary and necessary expenses for your self‑employment)? Or are those unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job? (If they are business expenses, they are deductible on Schedule C.)
2. Was the $2,700 in student‑loan interest paid during 2023? (Only interest paid in 2023 matters.) Do you have a 1098‑E from MOHELA for 2023?
3. Do you receive any 1099‑NEC forms for the consulting payments? (If not, you still must report the income.)
4. Do you pay any significant Arizona state income tax or other deductible state/local taxes that would meaningfully increase itemized deductions? (Helps check itemize vs standard.)

If you want, I can:
- Recalculate an exact AGI and show whether the student‑loan deduction phases out once you confirm the 3 items above.
- Prepare a checklist of entries for your tax software or provide step‑by‑step where to enter each item on Form 1040 and the Schedules.

Which would you like me to do next — run the quick AGI/deduction calculation if you confirm whether the $1,350 are consulting business expenses and whether the $2,700 was actually paid in 2023?

